1
00:00:59,647 --> 00:01:00,647
WOMAN: Help him!

2
00:01:00,847 --> 00:01:02,506
Ambulance is here now.
They'll take care of him.

3
00:01:02,507 --> 00:01:03,507
(WOMAN SHRIEKING)

4
00:01:03,617 --> 00:01:04,746
There's been a blow to the head.

5
00:01:04,747 --> 00:01:07,159
She's been doing CPR
for about 15 minutes.

6
00:01:07,517 --> 00:01:08,917
Mum, please, Mum.

7
00:01:09,187 --> 00:01:11,497
It's all right. Dad will be all right.

8
00:01:13,127 --> 00:01:14,539
- I'm so, so...
- No, no.

9
00:01:14,587 --> 00:01:15,587
(SHUSHING)

10
00:01:16,057 --> 00:01:17,577
We'll be all right.

11
00:01:23,267 --> 00:01:26,506
You have that horrible James Brooklands
back in the list, Judge.

12
00:01:26,507 --> 00:01:27,767
He's here, is he?

13
00:01:28,037 --> 00:01:30,676
They had him brought from the remand
centre to await your pleasure.

14
00:01:30,677 --> 00:01:32,736
Mr Petros, the QC defending Brooklands,

15
00:01:32,737 --> 00:01:34,946
is complaining about his client
being in custody.

16
00:01:34,947 --> 00:01:38,047
Yes, well, remand was never meant
as a punishment.

17
00:01:39,147 --> 00:01:42,416
Perhaps it's given Mr Brooklands
the opportunity to reflect long and hard

18
00:01:42,417 --> 00:01:44,756
on the mother and two children
that he ran over.

19
00:01:44,757 --> 00:01:47,417
I doubt it.
Here, Mimi, do you want a nut?

20
00:01:49,127 --> 00:01:51,907
- Apparently not.
- Oh, yes, you do.

21
00:01:56,697 --> 00:01:57,717
Judge?

22
00:01:59,067 --> 00:02:00,950
Can you start a judicial review?

23
00:02:01,267 --> 00:02:03,487
It's not how it usually happens, no.

24
00:02:04,707 --> 00:02:07,376
The company my brother works for
has been taken over.

25
00:02:07,377 --> 00:02:10,613
Bracewright China.
They're major manufacturers of china.

26
00:02:11,047 --> 00:02:13,786
The first thing the company
that took them over did

27
00:02:13,787 --> 00:02:15,567
was raid the pension fund.

28
00:02:16,517 --> 00:02:18,916
There's legislation in place
to prevent that.

29
00:02:18,917 --> 00:02:20,212
Well, it doesn't work.

30
00:02:20,357 --> 00:02:24,005
The DTI says firms mustn't be burdened
with extra restrictions,

31
00:02:24,427 --> 00:02:26,957
or there will be no
entrepreneurial society.

32
00:02:27,667 --> 00:02:29,397
It's a bit high<i>-</i>handed.

33
00:02:29,697 --> 00:02:31,336
What happened to your brother's pension?

34
00:02:31,337 --> 00:02:33,536
Well, he was expecting
about ￡100 a week.

35
00:02:33,537 --> 00:02:35,757
He'll be lucky if he gets ￡5.

36
00:02:36,137 --> 00:02:38,247
DTI's response could be reviewed.

37
00:02:39,277 --> 00:02:43,042
- He's very angry about it.
- It's not a cheap process, you know.

38
00:02:43,517 --> 00:02:45,797
Would Mrs Mills see him, do you think?

39
00:02:46,317 --> 00:02:48,247
No, not without a solicitor.

40
00:02:51,387 --> 00:02:54,093
Yes, yes, all right.
I'll have a word with her.

41
00:02:54,157 --> 00:02:56,826
I suppose I could see Coop's brother,
I've nothing else.

42
00:02:56,827 --> 00:02:59,167
Suddenly I'm no one's favourite QC.

43
00:02:59,597 --> 00:03:02,597
- You're still top of my list.
- That's the problem.

44
00:03:03,237 --> 00:03:06,606
It all comes from your helping me
at the disciplinary tribunal.

45
00:03:06,607 --> 00:03:08,466
If I hadn't,
you'd be off the road by now.

46
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:12,703
Oh, not necessarily. And anyway,
whose fault was that in the first place?

47
00:03:13,277 --> 00:03:16,176
So we should regularise our relationship
and get married.

48
00:03:16,177 --> 00:03:18,487
- John, that's not where it's at.
- Why?

49
00:03:19,617 --> 00:03:21,559
Why? You've always been my ideal.

50
00:03:21,717 --> 00:03:24,070
You're everything that a man could want.

51
00:03:24,217 --> 00:03:25,267
Bright.

52
00:03:25,957 --> 00:03:27,097
Beautiful.

53
00:03:27,627 --> 00:03:28,707
Compassionate.

54
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:31,387
Being your ideal is too much
of a burden.

55
00:03:32,657 --> 00:03:35,226
Anyway, five minutes into marriage,
you'd be off chasing other women.

56
00:03:35,227 --> 00:03:36,466
No, I wouldn't. Scout's honour.

57
00:03:36,467 --> 00:03:38,115
All right, then, 10 minutes.

58
00:03:38,197 --> 00:03:40,406
You love the idea
of being in love with me

59
00:03:40,407 --> 00:03:42,367
because I keep my distance.

60
00:03:42,807 --> 00:03:44,506
Real relationships are hard work.

61
00:03:44,507 --> 00:03:46,272
It's much easier to walk away.

62
00:03:46,507 --> 00:03:48,506
The other person always promises
something better.

63
00:03:48,507 --> 00:03:49,946
There is no one better. I know that.

64
00:03:49,947 --> 00:03:52,167
No, you just think you do.

65
00:03:55,617 --> 00:03:57,167
You need help, John.

66
00:03:58,287 --> 00:04:00,067
All right. I'll get help.

67
00:04:07,167 --> 00:04:09,896
Are you sure you're serious
about therapy, Sir John?

68
00:04:09,897 --> 00:04:11,896
As long as it's not in front
of your students.

69
00:04:11,897 --> 00:04:14,545
I don't have students.
I lecture occasionally.

70
00:04:15,207 --> 00:04:17,036
What do you do the rest of the time?

71
00:04:17,037 --> 00:04:18,937
I usually ask the questions.

72
00:04:20,947 --> 00:04:23,242
Yes, barristers often complain of that.

73
00:04:23,547 --> 00:04:26,127
They say I examine the witnesses
too much.

74
00:04:26,747 --> 00:04:29,042
Do you like being in control, Sir John?

75
00:04:29,117 --> 00:04:30,756
Is this part of the therapy?

76
00:04:30,757 --> 00:04:32,507
Everything has a bearing.

77
00:04:33,157 --> 00:04:34,956
Does this mean we're getting started?

78
00:04:34,957 --> 00:04:37,327
I have to be convinced you're serious.

79
00:04:38,157 --> 00:04:40,766
Could we explore this over dinner,
do you think?

80
00:04:40,767 --> 00:04:42,377
Is that against the rules?

81
00:04:42,667 --> 00:04:45,020
I never have social contact
with clients.

82
00:04:45,367 --> 00:04:46,567
Neither do I.

83
00:04:49,007 --> 00:04:51,117
What is it you want out of this?

84
00:04:52,337 --> 00:04:54,887
To be able to form
a long<i>-</i>term relationship.

85
00:04:55,507 --> 00:04:57,146
I don't have a magic formula.

86
00:04:57,147 --> 00:04:59,727
Then what do therapists
do for their money?

87
00:05:00,617 --> 00:05:03,912
Who is it you want to have
a long<i>-</i>term relationship with?

88
00:05:03,987 --> 00:05:08,877
The Lord Chancellor and his aperient,
Ian Rochester, would be a start.

89
00:05:11,127 --> 00:05:14,166
Okay. Call my office tomorrow,
we'll make an appointment.

90
00:05:14,167 --> 00:05:16,266
James Brooklands remaining on remand

91
00:05:16,267 --> 00:05:18,503
is a clear breach of his human rights.

92
00:05:18,637 --> 00:05:20,807
Does it embarrass your masters, Ian?

93
00:05:21,207 --> 00:05:23,077
That is not a consideration.

94
00:05:23,477 --> 00:05:26,106
Are you trying to push me off
the Brooklands case?

95
00:05:26,107 --> 00:05:28,417
As a champion of the European system,

96
00:05:28,847 --> 00:05:32,142
I thought you'd argue for not keeping
this man on remand.

97
00:05:33,177 --> 00:05:35,957
Well, I...
I will get to the case quite soon.

98
00:05:36,617 --> 00:05:39,147
We'd rather you took
the Dobbs murder trial.

99
00:05:39,217 --> 00:05:42,426
It's straightforward enough.
Why not give it to a purple judge?

100
00:05:42,427 --> 00:05:44,157
There's none available.

101
00:05:45,857 --> 00:05:49,152
The overwhelming burden of evidence
is against Mrs Dobbs,

102
00:05:49,567 --> 00:05:52,817
but it's not altogether certain
she did the slaying.

103
00:05:53,867 --> 00:05:55,857
Then why is she going to trial?

104
00:05:56,537 --> 00:05:59,836
The DPP was rather hoping you'd
do one of your famous interventions

105
00:05:59,837 --> 00:06:01,576
to penetrate to the heart of the matter.

106
00:06:01,577 --> 00:06:03,417
Is that how it works, Ian?

107
00:06:03,747 --> 00:06:07,647
Either way, the Dobbs trial
is on your list ahead of Brooklands.

108
00:06:08,287 --> 00:06:10,427
Unless you specifically refuse.

109
00:06:11,757 --> 00:06:15,707
Which leaves the problem of what to do
with Brooklands, meanwhile.

110
00:06:21,897 --> 00:06:23,827
- Will you grant him bail?
- No.

111
00:06:27,367 --> 00:06:29,507
It's entirely in your hands, John.

112
00:06:33,107 --> 00:06:37,402
There's no doubt Deed lied to the
disciplinary tribunal, I suppose, Monty.

113
00:06:37,447 --> 00:06:39,287
It was clear and blatant.

114
00:06:39,647 --> 00:06:41,589
But he's a High Court judge, Ian.

115
00:06:41,887 --> 00:06:44,299
If he said he didn't sleep
with Mrs Mills,

116
00:06:44,557 --> 00:06:45,916
we have to accept his word.

117
00:06:45,917 --> 00:06:47,907
I took that view, Michael.

118
00:06:49,157 --> 00:06:51,817
We have photographs of them in bed.

119
00:06:54,327 --> 00:06:55,916
But how were they obtained?

120
00:06:56,097 --> 00:06:58,392
An overzealous servant at the lodgings.

121
00:06:58,767 --> 00:07:00,727
Is that fair, Ian?

122
00:07:01,067 --> 00:07:05,362
We're dealing with a judge who seems
to pay no heed to the rules, Michael.

123
00:07:06,947 --> 00:07:09,276
You're unusually silent, Joe.
Is this difficult for you,

124
00:07:09,277 --> 00:07:11,007
being his ex<i>-</i>father<i>-</i>in<i>-</i>law?

125
00:07:11,317 --> 00:07:14,847
As much as I dislike Deed,
I've never found him to be a liar.

126
00:07:15,087 --> 00:07:18,617
Quite the reverse. He's always
too truthful for his own good.

127
00:07:18,717 --> 00:07:20,907
The evidence speaks for itself, Joe.

128
00:07:21,387 --> 00:07:23,326
It's meaningless. We couldn't use it.

129
00:07:23,327 --> 00:07:25,667
To do so would be to imbrute the bench.

130
00:07:27,067 --> 00:07:29,420
It does rather leave us
in a cleft stick.

131
00:07:29,797 --> 00:07:31,987
Something has to be done about him.

132
00:07:32,797 --> 00:07:34,607
There's nothing one can do.

133
00:07:36,537 --> 00:07:39,147
We could put more pressure
on Mrs Mills, Joe.

134
00:07:39,637 --> 00:07:41,446
She was his co<i>-</i>conspirator in this.

135
00:07:41,447 --> 00:07:43,846
But I thought that was the reason
that she went before

136
00:07:43,847 --> 00:07:46,116
the disciplinary tribunal
in the first place.

137
00:07:46,117 --> 00:07:48,547
We've been altogether too nice, Michael.

138
00:07:50,157 --> 00:07:53,216
I would suggest a less confrontational
approach, Ian.

139
00:07:54,827 --> 00:07:57,377
Have a quiet word
with the Attorney General.

140
00:07:58,157 --> 00:08:00,677
By controlling the cases,

141
00:08:01,127 --> 00:08:02,677
that go to each of them,

142
00:08:02,997 --> 00:08:07,387
Deed and Mrs Mills
might be marginalized.

143
00:08:09,607 --> 00:08:11,636
We thought it might be useful
to take your mind

144
00:08:11,637 --> 00:08:12,906
on the discussion document

145
00:08:12,907 --> 00:08:15,790
for a continental<i>-</i>style
Ministry of Justice, Alan.

146
00:08:15,847 --> 00:08:17,776
I thought the Lord Chancellor
was against it.

147
00:08:17,777 --> 00:08:19,617
Oh, we remain implacable.

148
00:08:19,947 --> 00:08:21,716
Deed gave rather a good paper on it

149
00:08:21,717 --> 00:08:25,129
to the European Human Rights seminar
at Warwick last month.

150
00:08:25,417 --> 00:08:29,653
Unlike politicians, High Court judges
don't have to clear their speeches.

151
00:08:29,857 --> 00:08:32,387
Yes, the director was complaining
about him.

152
00:08:32,427 --> 00:08:34,066
And the CPS are a bit fed up with him

153
00:08:34,067 --> 00:08:36,677
taking the prosecution
and defence in cases.

154
00:08:39,067 --> 00:08:41,437
Deed can be very useful sometimes, Ian.

155
00:08:42,207 --> 00:08:45,502
Does that mean you couldn't
support any move against him?

156
00:08:45,537 --> 00:08:46,997
Not necessarily.

157
00:08:47,877 --> 00:08:49,760
Do you have a specific proposal?

158
00:08:49,907 --> 00:08:52,672
We'd like to close His Lordship
out of the loop,

159
00:08:52,747 --> 00:08:54,937
along with his paramour, Mrs Mills.

160
00:09:00,727 --> 00:09:02,127
(ALL CHATTERING)

161
00:09:08,367 --> 00:09:10,477
- Michael?
- Ah, Jo.

162
00:09:10,897 --> 00:09:13,666
I had dinner with John Deed
the other night. Very good form.

163
00:09:13,667 --> 00:09:15,507
Being a judge so suits him.

164
00:09:16,437 --> 00:09:18,167
I need your support, Michael.

165
00:09:18,477 --> 00:09:21,706
CPS have bumped me from my murder case
in favour of Newman Mason Allen.

166
00:09:21,707 --> 00:09:23,287
They're the client, Jo.

167
00:09:23,577 --> 00:09:25,146
They briefed me for the prosecution.

168
00:09:25,147 --> 00:09:27,246
I can't tell them where to place work.

169
00:09:27,247 --> 00:09:29,542
As Head of Chambers, you could protest.

170
00:09:31,517 --> 00:09:34,556
I know we have sweetheart relationships
with the CPS,

171
00:09:34,557 --> 00:09:37,107
but I did nothing wrong, Michael.

172
00:09:37,557 --> 00:09:39,517
No one imagines otherwise, Jo.

173
00:09:41,127 --> 00:09:43,696
Aren't we getting a little too dependent
on the CPS?

174
00:09:43,697 --> 00:09:46,462
You and Newman and Julia
practically live there.

175
00:09:46,597 --> 00:09:48,506
We have to manage a balance of work, Jo.

176
00:09:48,507 --> 00:09:51,037
Prosecuting or defending,
it's all the same.

177
00:09:51,237 --> 00:09:52,437
Excuse me.

178
00:09:55,307 --> 00:09:57,477
(PEOPLE CHATTERING)

179
00:10:12,127 --> 00:10:13,937
Am I on my way out of here?

180
00:10:15,067 --> 00:10:16,826
We're working hard on your behalf.

181
00:10:16,827 --> 00:10:18,997
- I'm still here, Clive.
- Yes.

182
00:10:19,837 --> 00:10:22,106
How would you feel
about pleading guilty?

183
00:10:22,107 --> 00:10:24,076
Look, I know I ran over those two girls
and their mother,

184
00:10:24,077 --> 00:10:26,037
but it was an accident, Alex.

185
00:10:26,477 --> 00:10:30,066
We're asking for an indication
of sentence, were you to plead.

186
00:10:30,107 --> 00:10:32,687
With the right note
of contrition, James,

187
00:10:33,277 --> 00:10:36,572
you might be able to walk away
without a prison sentence.

188
00:10:38,287 --> 00:10:39,717
Well, is that possible?

189
00:10:39,987 --> 00:10:42,477
John, we're getting
into grave difficulties

190
00:10:42,927 --> 00:10:44,626
with James Brooklands coming back.

191
00:10:44,627 --> 00:10:46,226
It's not that much of a problem, is it?

192
00:10:46,227 --> 00:10:49,037
With you tied up
on the Dobbs murder trial?

193
00:10:49,527 --> 00:10:51,296
Brooklands can be further remanded.

194
00:10:51,297 --> 00:10:54,866
Well, personally, I'd sooner see him
go down and do some real time,

195
00:10:54,867 --> 00:10:56,917
but not before he's tried.

196
00:10:57,407 --> 00:10:59,290
What if he were to plead guilty?

197
00:10:59,607 --> 00:11:02,196
What would your indication
as to sentence be?

198
00:11:02,247 --> 00:11:05,876
I mean, you could interpose the guilty
plea and get it out of the way.

199
00:11:05,877 --> 00:11:10,113
- I'd want to give him the maximum.
- I'm sure he wouldn't agree to that.

200
00:11:10,117 --> 00:11:13,586
- Couldn't you let him out on bail?
- No, there's a risk he'll flee.

201
00:11:13,587 --> 00:11:16,626
His defence team have asked me
to hear an application

202
00:11:16,627 --> 00:11:18,596
for the trial to start immediately.

203
00:11:18,597 --> 00:11:20,026
Failing that, they want him released

204
00:11:20,027 --> 00:11:22,397
under Article 6
of the Human Rights Act.

205
00:11:23,167 --> 00:11:25,096
You could turn down the application.

206
00:11:25,097 --> 00:11:27,966
If I do that, they'll sue
for a writ of habeas corpus.

207
00:11:27,967 --> 00:11:31,732
You might have to release the case,
as you're not ready to start.

208
00:11:35,647 --> 00:11:38,353
Well, I don't want to
appear vindictive, Monty,

209
00:11:38,747 --> 00:11:40,386
but Brooklands had better go to somebody

210
00:11:40,387 --> 00:11:42,858
who'll deal with him
as the public expects.

211
00:11:43,187 --> 00:11:46,070
Yes, we'll see that
he's dealt with appropriately.

212
00:11:50,997 --> 00:11:52,077
JO: Are you alone?

213
00:11:52,327 --> 00:11:54,377
I think Mimi's around somewhere.

214
00:11:54,767 --> 00:11:56,347
Why? Would it matter?

215
00:11:56,967 --> 00:11:59,137
When are you seeing your therapist?

216
00:11:59,797 --> 00:12:00,966
What's the problem, Jo?

217
00:12:00,967 --> 00:12:03,266
Why should I have a problem?
Do you know of one?

218
00:12:03,267 --> 00:12:05,436
Why does everyone assume
that I have a problem?

219
00:12:05,437 --> 00:12:07,908
I was cleared
by the disciplinary tribunal.

220
00:12:08,247 --> 00:12:09,387
All right.

221
00:12:09,847 --> 00:12:12,495
- Relax. Have a drink.
- I don't want a drink.

222
00:12:13,717 --> 00:12:15,586
I want to know what's happening.

223
00:12:15,587 --> 00:12:17,486
Could you be a little bit more specific?

224
00:12:17,487 --> 00:12:19,627
- I've been dropped by the CPS.
- Oh.

225
00:12:20,757 --> 00:12:22,627
Well, it happens, you know.

226
00:12:22,957 --> 00:12:25,899
- Twice in the same week?
- Did they give a reason?

227
00:12:26,227 --> 00:12:29,169
They thought it better
if I wasn't in front of you.

228
00:12:29,767 --> 00:12:32,137
I don't see that
as a serious impediment.

229
00:12:33,837 --> 00:12:36,706
When was it decided that you were
to hear the Dobbs murder trial?

230
00:12:36,707 --> 00:12:39,178
Earlier today.
It was switched in the list.

231
00:12:39,307 --> 00:12:41,177
I was dumped two days ago.

232
00:12:41,947 --> 00:12:45,359
It wasn't until I complained today
that you were mentioned.

233
00:12:45,417 --> 00:12:48,116
Well, somebody's been had,
and I don't think it's you.

234
00:12:48,117 --> 00:12:50,000
It was me who lost the CPS work.

235
00:12:50,287 --> 00:12:54,346
Of course, no one in chambers will
protest because they might be next.

236
00:12:55,027 --> 00:12:57,296
Do you think you might be making
a fuss over nothing?

237
00:12:57,297 --> 00:12:59,727
It wasn't you who got the sack.

238
00:13:01,227 --> 00:13:03,836
Would you like me to have a word
with your head of chambers?

239
00:13:03,837 --> 00:13:04,887
Absolutely not.

240
00:13:05,137 --> 00:13:06,696
I'm fed up with it
being such a boys' club.

241
00:13:06,697 --> 00:13:09,286
Even Julia Crampsie behaves
like one of them.

242
00:13:09,607 --> 00:13:11,636
- Nothing I can do about that.
- Oh, I don't want you to.

243
00:13:11,637 --> 00:13:13,657
Well, what do you want me to do?

244
00:13:14,207 --> 00:13:16,325
You don't want to go to bed with me.

245
00:13:18,247 --> 00:13:19,746
You think that's the solution
to everything.

246
00:13:19,747 --> 00:13:20,887
Isn't it?

247
00:13:30,997 --> 00:13:33,056
- Do you want to stay and eat?
- No.

248
00:13:33,727 --> 00:13:35,597
- Jo.
- Forget it.

249
00:13:42,707 --> 00:13:44,806
Coop's brother should go
for judicial review.

250
00:13:44,807 --> 00:13:48,346
The DTI's indifference to the workers
losing their pensions is outrageous.

251
00:13:48,347 --> 00:13:50,276
You can't make a case
out of their indifference.

252
00:13:50,277 --> 00:13:53,237
The DTI took a clear position. Tough.

253
00:13:53,717 --> 00:13:55,186
Does he have the money to go to law?

254
00:13:55,187 --> 00:13:58,086
The short answer is no,
but too much to qualify for legal aid.

255
00:13:58,087 --> 00:14:01,657
And are you going to take it on?

256
00:14:02,387 --> 00:14:03,997
I'll help where I can.

257
00:14:04,797 --> 00:14:06,445
- Will you support me?
- How?

258
00:14:06,597 --> 00:14:08,866
It might be difficult
because of my relationship with Coop.

259
00:14:08,867 --> 00:14:10,596
To say nothing of our relationship.

260
00:14:10,597 --> 00:14:12,396
Well, we haven't got one,
according to you.

261
00:14:12,397 --> 00:14:14,767
But you do try, though, John.

262
00:14:16,107 --> 00:14:18,636
MASON ALLEN: Can you tell the court
what you found

263
00:14:18,637 --> 00:14:20,806
when you arrived
at the defendant's home?

264
00:14:20,807 --> 00:14:23,706
MILBROOK: She was giving her husband
mouth<i>-</i>to<i>-</i>mouth resuscitation.

265
00:14:23,707 --> 00:14:25,667
Was the victim alive or dead?

266
00:14:26,047 --> 00:14:28,767
Dead. Well, leastwise I couldn't
find a pulse.

267
00:14:29,947 --> 00:14:32,386
- Do you know how long he'd been dead?
- Fifteen minutes.

268
00:14:32,387 --> 00:14:34,426
Do you know that or are you speculating?

269
00:14:34,427 --> 00:14:36,386
I didn't know at the time,
but it was confirmed later

270
00:14:36,387 --> 00:14:38,026
that the first blow killed him.

271
00:14:38,027 --> 00:14:39,867
I see. Mr Mason Allen.

272
00:14:41,197 --> 00:14:45,166
Do you know for how long Mrs Dobbs had
been trying to resuscitate her husband?

273
00:14:45,167 --> 00:14:47,007
- Not long.
- How do you know?

274
00:14:47,367 --> 00:14:49,806
It's knackering,
especially if they're not coming back.

275
00:14:49,807 --> 00:14:51,676
You couldn't keep it up for 15 minutes.

276
00:14:51,677 --> 00:14:55,577
So it's likely that Mrs Dobbs
assumed this position

277
00:14:56,207 --> 00:14:58,855
with your arrival
to give a caring impression.

278
00:14:58,947 --> 00:15:01,686
My lord, my learned friend
is calling for speculation

279
00:15:01,687 --> 00:15:02,946
on the part of this witness.

280
00:15:02,947 --> 00:15:06,065
Yes, I'm more concerned
that he's leading the witness.

281
00:15:06,787 --> 00:15:08,127
I do apologise.

282
00:15:09,287 --> 00:15:11,917
Officer, had Mrs Dobbs

283
00:15:12,397 --> 00:15:15,126
been trying to revive her husband
for 15 minutes?

284
00:15:15,127 --> 00:15:17,237
No, she didn't look like she had.

285
00:15:17,967 --> 00:15:21,127
- Was she exhausted or sweating?
- No.

286
00:15:21,837 --> 00:15:23,836
- What was her son doing?
- Nothing.

287
00:15:23,837 --> 00:15:25,447
He was in a state of shock.

288
00:15:25,777 --> 00:15:28,954
- Well, who admitted you to the house?
- The boy, Andy.

289
00:15:29,277 --> 00:15:31,147
Did he say anything to you?

290
00:15:32,417 --> 00:15:34,947
"It's my dad, it's my dad.
They killed him."

291
00:15:34,987 --> 00:15:37,635
Meaning some burglars
who'd entered the house.

292
00:15:37,717 --> 00:15:40,377
You're sure he said, "They killed him"?

293
00:15:40,857 --> 00:15:42,326
That was the note I took down.

294
00:15:42,327 --> 00:15:44,326
Remind us when you made this note.

295
00:15:44,327 --> 00:15:47,326
When I got back to the station, sir,
about an hour later.

296
00:15:47,327 --> 00:15:48,787
An hour, I see.

297
00:15:49,867 --> 00:15:52,297
How would you describe the boy?

298
00:15:53,197 --> 00:15:56,206
In the face of his father
having been bludgeoned to death?

299
00:15:56,207 --> 00:15:57,577
Mr Mason Allen.

300
00:15:58,437 --> 00:16:00,397
I do apologise, my lord.

301
00:16:01,847 --> 00:16:06,157
Um, one further point, Sergeant.

302
00:16:11,387 --> 00:16:13,667
Mr Justice Everard has taken the case.

303
00:16:14,087 --> 00:16:16,756
He's arranged for the charge
against Brooklands to revert

304
00:16:16,757 --> 00:16:19,067
to causing death by dangerous driving.

305
00:16:20,027 --> 00:16:22,796
- When did this happen, Coop?
- Well, I've just heard.

306
00:16:22,797 --> 00:16:23,797
Nol

307
00:16:25,397 --> 00:16:26,809
It must be manslaughter.

308
00:16:27,067 --> 00:16:29,479
I knew that's
what you recommended, Judge.

309
00:16:29,567 --> 00:16:31,920
If they think they've
outmanoeuvred me...

310
00:16:32,007 --> 00:16:35,419
Brooklands could walk free,
especially if he pleads guilty.

311
00:16:36,047 --> 00:16:38,047
I'll talk to the Attorney General.

312
00:16:38,147 --> 00:16:41,086
What do you expect from me?
The CPS determines the charges.

313
00:16:41,087 --> 00:16:43,323
With a little help from Monty Everard.

314
00:16:43,917 --> 00:16:45,986
Oh, I could have a word
with the director.

315
00:16:45,987 --> 00:16:46,987
I'm not inclined.

316
00:16:47,187 --> 00:16:49,286
Plea bargaining's
a loathsome process, Alan.

317
00:16:49,287 --> 00:16:51,337
Families of victims feel betrayed.

318
00:16:51,727 --> 00:16:53,926
If it happens with Brooklands,
it'll be a travesty.

319
00:16:53,927 --> 00:16:55,737
You're overreacting, Sir John.

320
00:16:56,067 --> 00:16:58,396
Sometimes the lesser charges
guarantee a conviction.

321
00:16:58,397 --> 00:17:01,280
Here it will guarantee
that Brooklands walks free.

322
00:17:02,007 --> 00:17:03,297
Oh, I don't know.

323
00:17:04,507 --> 00:17:07,096
Isn't this something
that might never happen?

324
00:17:07,807 --> 00:17:08,977
If it does,

325
00:17:10,107 --> 00:17:11,402
there will be trouble.

326
00:17:17,457 --> 00:17:20,926
My lord, the prosecution having amended
the charge against Mr Brooklands

327
00:17:20,927 --> 00:17:23,177
to causing death by dangerous driving,

328
00:17:23,557 --> 00:17:26,296
my client would wish
to have the indictment put again.

329
00:17:26,297 --> 00:17:29,867
As long as he realises
this is still a serious charge.

330
00:17:32,867 --> 00:17:34,357
James Brooklands,

331
00:17:34,637 --> 00:17:37,637
on the charge of causing death
by dangerous driving,

332
00:17:38,077 --> 00:17:39,776
that on the 15th of March last,

333
00:17:39,777 --> 00:17:41,976
you caused a motor vehicle
you were driving

334
00:17:41,977 --> 00:17:43,267
to go out of control,

335
00:17:43,507 --> 00:17:46,757
killing Alison and Liza Cox
and their mother, Vanessa.

336
00:17:47,887 --> 00:17:50,257
How do you plead? Guilty or not guilty?

337
00:17:51,387 --> 00:17:52,497
Guilty.

338
00:17:54,257 --> 00:17:58,375
Is there anything you wish to say
in mitigation before I pass sentence?

339
00:17:58,527 --> 00:18:02,586
Well, I don't know what I could say
to the family of the victims, sir.

340
00:18:02,867 --> 00:18:05,220
If I could bring them back
with some act,

341
00:18:05,237 --> 00:18:08,017
some personal sacrifice,

342
00:18:09,467 --> 00:18:11,337
I'd give away my business.

343
00:18:12,507 --> 00:18:14,237
Even give away my life.

344
00:18:16,207 --> 00:18:18,137
Their deaths have haunted me.

345
00:18:20,017 --> 00:18:21,886
If you were to put me in prison
for the rest of my life,

346
00:18:21,887 --> 00:18:24,227
I still couldn't escape what I'd done.

347
00:18:25,587 --> 00:18:28,786
I shouldn't have been talking
on the phone while I was driving.

348
00:18:28,787 --> 00:18:30,097
No one should.

349
00:18:31,197 --> 00:18:32,796
I've written to every member
of Parliament.

350
00:18:32,797 --> 00:18:35,166
I'm lobbying
all the influential people I know

351
00:18:35,167 --> 00:18:36,977
to get the law changed.

352
00:18:37,327 --> 00:18:40,210
Something should result from this...
This tragedy.

353
00:18:41,207 --> 00:18:43,197
I couldn't bear it if it doesn't.

354
00:18:43,807 --> 00:18:46,749
We are pleased to note your remorse,
Mr Brooklands.

355
00:18:47,007 --> 00:18:49,467
It would be uncharitable to ignore it.

356
00:18:49,877 --> 00:18:52,537
- My lord...
- Now, I've read all the reports

357
00:18:53,017 --> 00:18:54,446
on the defendant, Mr Gaydon.

358
00:18:54,447 --> 00:18:57,116
- Yes, but I...
- I don't need to see anything more

359
00:18:57,117 --> 00:18:58,817
before I pass sentence.

360
00:19:01,457 --> 00:19:02,747
Mr Brooklands.

361
00:19:03,957 --> 00:19:07,267
This is a charge that warrants
a long prison sentence.

362
00:19:08,727 --> 00:19:12,447
However, it is clear
that you have suffered greatly

363
00:19:13,197 --> 00:19:14,807
while being remanded.

364
00:19:15,337 --> 00:19:19,106
I imagine the thought of going
back to prison fills you with terror.

365
00:19:19,107 --> 00:19:20,947
Yes, sir, it terrifies me.

366
00:19:22,047 --> 00:19:24,507
Then prison has served a useful purpose.

367
00:19:25,677 --> 00:19:29,957
I sentence you to what will amount
to time served on remand.

368
00:19:30,647 --> 00:19:32,107
But added to this,

369
00:19:32,717 --> 00:19:35,586
I'm going to give you
the maximum sentence in the community.

370
00:19:35,587 --> 00:19:37,807
Two hundred and forty hours service.

371
00:19:38,557 --> 00:19:39,787
Release him.

372
00:19:42,427 --> 00:19:43,827
USHER: All rise.

373
00:19:58,247 --> 00:20:00,907
Wretch. You miserable wretch.

374
00:20:01,417 --> 00:20:04,457
- You are out of control.
- You're impotent.

375
00:20:09,587 --> 00:20:11,987
And you, sir, are a barefaced liar.

376
00:20:12,397 --> 00:20:13,796
What do you think you're doing
in my court?

377
00:20:13,797 --> 00:20:15,739
Witnessing how spineless you are.

378
00:20:15,927 --> 00:20:17,196
You make me ashamed of the bench.

379
00:20:17,197 --> 00:20:19,807
Ah, and what are
you doing to do? Resign?

380
00:20:20,237 --> 00:20:22,366
No, I'm going to talk
to the Attorney General again,

381
00:20:22,367 --> 00:20:25,779
have him appeal your sentence
and stick your crony in jail.

382
00:20:26,177 --> 00:20:27,847
I spoke to him earlier.

383
00:20:28,307 --> 00:20:31,147
Sir Alan sees Brooklands
as a wealth creator.

384
00:20:31,717 --> 00:20:33,797
One who shouldn't be in prison.

385
00:20:39,857 --> 00:20:42,857
Is there nothing you can do
about Brooklands, Judge?

386
00:20:43,327 --> 00:20:45,637
Not unless he kills somebody else, no.

387
00:20:46,627 --> 00:20:49,216
It's Justice Everard
and the Attorney General

388
00:20:49,367 --> 00:20:51,557
I should like to do something about.

389
00:20:56,367 --> 00:20:59,132
I'm gonna talk to the <i>'Times'</i>
law correspondent.

390
00:21:00,007 --> 00:21:02,713
Let's see how comfortable
that makes them feel.

391
00:21:05,717 --> 00:21:10,057
The Brooklands case represents the very
heart of cronyism in our system.

392
00:21:11,457 --> 00:21:15,222
We all tend to favour our own,
of course. It's only human nature.

393
00:21:15,587 --> 00:21:17,837
Politicians and political appointees

394
00:21:18,297 --> 00:21:21,180
will tend to favour the likes
of James Brooklands.

395
00:21:21,527 --> 00:21:23,867
They make huge donations to the party.

396
00:21:24,797 --> 00:21:27,317
What do you feel is the solution,
Sir John?

397
00:21:28,237 --> 00:21:29,885
Well, that's a big question.

398
00:21:31,607 --> 00:21:34,960
Not having politics funded
by donations in the first place

399
00:21:35,407 --> 00:21:36,777
would be a start.

400
00:21:37,047 --> 00:21:39,416
Having independent government
Law Officers

401
00:21:39,417 --> 00:21:42,300
instead of political apparatchiks
would also help.

402
00:21:43,047 --> 00:21:46,387
You see,
politicians aren't the consciousness.

403
00:21:47,687 --> 00:21:49,937
They only reflect our consciousness.

404
00:21:50,587 --> 00:21:52,897
Do you intend to resign now, Sir John?

405
00:21:54,397 --> 00:21:55,397
No.

406
00:21:55,697 --> 00:21:58,933
I intend to stay and fight
the administrative corruption

407
00:21:59,237 --> 00:22:03,179
displayed by Mr Justice Everard
and his cohorts in Court Number One.

408
00:22:03,667 --> 00:22:05,947
- Can I quote that directly?
- Yes.

409
00:22:07,847 --> 00:22:10,789
Everything that I have said
has been on the record.

410
00:22:11,817 --> 00:22:13,416
It's been a poor day for justice.

411
00:22:13,417 --> 00:22:15,997
If you're rich and keep on making money,

412
00:22:17,087 --> 00:22:18,617
then you're above the law.

413
00:22:21,087 --> 00:22:22,377
Look at these.

414
00:22:23,487 --> 00:22:27,426
Unless we achieve some effective
damage limitation very fast, Laurence,

415
00:22:27,427 --> 00:22:30,663
one or both of us are liable
to be transferred to Defra.

416
00:22:30,767 --> 00:22:32,062
Or worse, Immigration.

417
00:22:33,197 --> 00:22:35,507
We could go on the counter<i>-</i>offensive.

418
00:22:36,667 --> 00:22:38,127
Do you have one?

419
00:22:39,377 --> 00:22:41,319
JO: Do you have a good bodyguard?

420
00:22:41,647 --> 00:22:44,706
- Do I need one?
<i>- I've just been reading your press</i>.

421
00:22:45,017 --> 00:22:46,537
You free for lunch?

422
00:22:54,057 --> 00:22:56,047
What's the problem, Mrs Dobbs?

423
00:22:56,957 --> 00:22:58,917
I've been thinking about Andy.

424
00:22:59,997 --> 00:23:01,487
I need to see him.

425
00:23:02,527 --> 00:23:04,697
You can't. He's a defence witness.

426
00:23:05,337 --> 00:23:07,147
I don't want him to be.

427
00:23:07,597 --> 00:23:08,857
He mustn't be.

428
00:23:09,137 --> 00:23:11,006
But he'll confirm what happened.

429
00:23:11,007 --> 00:23:12,836
Without him, you could be facing
a murder conviction.

430
00:23:12,837 --> 00:23:15,602
If that happens,
there can be only one sentence.

431
00:23:15,807 --> 00:23:17,267
Life imprisonment.

432
00:23:18,177 --> 00:23:19,697
I don't care.

433
00:23:21,887 --> 00:23:24,197
I don't want him to suffer any more.

434
00:23:24,617 --> 00:23:27,137
I can't bear what this is doing to him.

435
00:23:28,287 --> 00:23:30,526
Mrs Dobbs,
I've talked at length with Andy.

436
00:23:30,527 --> 00:23:32,607
He's a very sensible young man.

437
00:23:33,897 --> 00:23:37,486
He'll do your case nothing but good.
Trust me on this, please.

438
00:23:45,037 --> 00:23:46,736
POWELL: Sergeant Milbrook,
you said in evidence

439
00:23:46,737 --> 00:23:49,706
that the room was orderly
with no signs of a struggle.

440
00:23:49,707 --> 00:23:51,547
That's right, there wasn't.

441
00:23:52,177 --> 00:23:53,746
Did you look anywhere else in the house?

442
00:23:53,747 --> 00:23:55,346
I did look in the other rooms, sir.

443
00:23:55,347 --> 00:23:57,416
There was no sign of a struggle
there, either.

444
00:23:57,417 --> 00:24:00,417
So your impression was of
a neat, orderly household?

445
00:24:00,457 --> 00:24:01,457
Yes.

446
00:24:01,687 --> 00:24:04,687
POWELL: Lived in by orderly,
restrained individuals.

447
00:24:05,057 --> 00:24:06,457
- Yes?
- Yes.

448
00:24:06,857 --> 00:24:08,637
I did get that impression.

449
00:24:09,227 --> 00:24:11,597
Have you ever rowed with your partner?

450
00:24:12,137 --> 00:24:13,137
Once or twice.

451
00:24:13,297 --> 00:24:15,533
Smashed the furniture, turned it over?

452
00:24:15,867 --> 00:24:17,537
No, I can't say we did.

453
00:24:17,837 --> 00:24:22,117
No. It's not the furniture we row with,
but another person.

454
00:24:23,807 --> 00:24:26,576
If someone was attacking you
and then turned on a child

455
00:24:26,577 --> 00:24:28,636
who was trying to stop that attack,

456
00:24:28,747 --> 00:24:32,806
and you ran to find something with which
to threaten him and stop him,

457
00:24:33,257 --> 00:24:36,486
there needn't necessarily be furniture
involved, need there?

458
00:24:36,487 --> 00:24:38,157
No, not really.

459
00:24:39,127 --> 00:24:41,126
Then, in those circumstances,
it wouldn't be surprising

460
00:24:41,127 --> 00:24:43,598
that the house
of three very orderly people

461
00:24:43,797 --> 00:24:45,847
- remained orderly, would it?
- No.

462
00:24:46,597 --> 00:24:49,362
- I suppose it wouldn't.
- Just as you found it.

463
00:24:54,707 --> 00:24:56,178
With your leave, my lord,

464
00:24:56,307 --> 00:24:59,131
I'd like to call
Detective Chief Inspector Hurst.

465
00:25:14,767 --> 00:25:16,907
It's totally scandalous, Ian.

466
00:25:17,297 --> 00:25:18,377
It's libellous.

467
00:25:18,597 --> 00:25:20,787
Well, we can't sue Deed for libel.

468
00:25:21,237 --> 00:25:23,120
I'd like to wring his damn neck.

469
00:25:23,237 --> 00:25:25,355
We mustn't give him the satisfaction

470
00:25:25,577 --> 00:25:26,930
of responding publicly.

471
00:25:27,137 --> 00:25:28,806
Well, then, do something about him.

472
00:25:28,807 --> 00:25:30,977
We can't swat him, Monty.

473
00:25:32,177 --> 00:25:35,086
The Lord Chancellor's meeting with
the Attorney and the Home Secretary

474
00:25:35,087 --> 00:25:36,970
to discuss how we might respond.

475
00:25:37,317 --> 00:25:39,286
Well, don't let him have any more cases.

476
00:25:39,287 --> 00:25:41,426
Alas, he has a number
reserved to himself.

477
00:25:41,427 --> 00:25:43,597
Then, we'll take them away from him.

478
00:25:44,527 --> 00:25:47,137
It's not just you
who got a bad press, Monty.

479
00:25:48,097 --> 00:25:50,677
We did, Court Services did and the CPS.

480
00:25:52,597 --> 00:25:55,266
You were altogether too lenient
with Brooklands.

481
00:25:55,267 --> 00:25:58,336
The man either makes money
for the country or he doesn't.

482
00:25:58,337 --> 00:26:00,117
He can't do it in prison.

483
00:26:02,807 --> 00:26:04,887
I'll speak with Deed when he rises.

484
00:26:05,247 --> 00:26:07,846
I don't want him on my circuit
a moment longer.

485
00:26:07,847 --> 00:26:09,746
If we can't stop him
hearing his reserve cases,

486
00:26:09,747 --> 00:26:12,042
then marginalise him
back at the Strand.

487
00:26:12,157 --> 00:26:14,569
Mightn't that be seen
to be punishing him?

488
00:26:14,887 --> 00:26:17,377
Since when was Central London
a punishment?

489
00:26:18,897 --> 00:26:20,647
I'll talk to him, Monty.

490
00:26:21,497 --> 00:26:24,086
Perhaps we'll put more pressure
on Mrs Mills.

491
00:26:30,807 --> 00:26:31,977
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

492
00:26:32,207 --> 00:26:33,437
Come in.

493
00:26:34,077 --> 00:26:35,597
Jo, how's it going?

494
00:26:36,247 --> 00:26:39,016
- I'm thinking of taking up knitting.
- Oh, dear.

495
00:26:39,017 --> 00:26:40,747
Still becalmed, are we?

496
00:26:41,787 --> 00:26:43,277
Well, um...

497
00:26:43,547 --> 00:26:46,067
I hate to be the bearer
of more bad news,

498
00:26:46,487 --> 00:26:51,027
but CPS are thinking of

499
00:26:52,357 --> 00:26:54,577
dropping you from their silks list.

500
00:26:58,797 --> 00:27:00,287
Did they say why?

501
00:27:00,567 --> 00:27:03,377
Oh, too many silks, I think.

502
00:27:03,837 --> 00:27:05,567
You don't believe that.

503
00:27:06,607 --> 00:27:09,846
I rather think John went over the top
with his public utterances.

504
00:27:09,847 --> 00:27:11,047
Don't you?

505
00:27:14,017 --> 00:27:15,767
Why don't we have lunch?

506
00:27:16,347 --> 00:27:18,097
Talk about your future.

507
00:27:18,757 --> 00:27:20,807
Are you free?

508
00:27:21,687 --> 00:27:23,647
Oh. No, I'm not, I'm afraid.

509
00:27:24,857 --> 00:27:27,526
Everard brought the bench
and the Lord Chancellor's department

510
00:27:27,527 --> 00:27:30,296
to the lowest and most shameful point
since before the abolition

511
00:27:30,297 --> 00:27:33,317
of capital punishment,
and you want me to apologise?

512
00:27:39,007 --> 00:27:40,617
The fact is, Sir John,

513
00:27:40,937 --> 00:27:44,526
judges don't normally criticise
their brethren. Not in public.

514
00:27:44,647 --> 00:27:47,412
The public expects judges
to be fair and decent,

515
00:27:47,517 --> 00:27:49,267
but above all, just.

516
00:27:50,287 --> 00:27:52,256
What I witnessed was so wantonly unjust,

517
00:27:52,257 --> 00:27:54,156
it cried out for a public statement.

518
00:27:54,157 --> 00:27:56,426
He should be exscinded
from alljudicial functions.

519
00:27:56,427 --> 00:27:59,696
And woe betide you if you or your
department had anything to do with it,

520
00:27:59,697 --> 00:28:01,526
because I'm gonna push and push on this

521
00:28:01,527 --> 00:28:03,426
until I get justice
for those two children

522
00:28:03,427 --> 00:28:04,797
and their mother.

523
00:28:05,097 --> 00:28:08,506
Yes, well, I'm sure the Lord Chancellor
will have something to say about that.

524
00:28:08,507 --> 00:28:10,736
He wants to see you
after adjournment tomorrow.

525
00:28:10,737 --> 00:28:12,502
No, I have another engagement.

526
00:28:14,177 --> 00:28:16,197
Is that wise, Sir John?

527
00:28:16,577 --> 00:28:18,357
Well, possibly not.

528
00:28:19,047 --> 00:28:21,016
But when you conspire
to behave corruptly,

529
00:28:21,017 --> 00:28:22,386
it has consequences for all of us.

530
00:28:22,387 --> 00:28:24,227
No one has behaved corruptly.

531
00:28:24,547 --> 00:28:25,686
Yes, well, that's not how it appears.

532
00:28:25,687 --> 00:28:28,426
Now, if you will excuse me,
I do have another appointment.

533
00:28:28,427 --> 00:28:30,597
There is one other thing, Sir John.

534
00:28:31,257 --> 00:28:34,846
Sir Monty is refusing to have you
sitting in his jurisdiction.

535
00:28:35,097 --> 00:28:36,297
(LAUGHING)

536
00:28:36,927 --> 00:28:40,057
Well, as the presider,
that is his right.

537
00:28:41,367 --> 00:28:44,027
You do have the power
to move me on elsewhere.

538
00:28:44,467 --> 00:28:45,577
But where?

539
00:28:48,277 --> 00:28:49,917
Yes. Well...

540
00:28:50,807 --> 00:28:54,216
Gosh, John, who are you meeting that
you turned down the Lord Chancellor?

541
00:28:54,217 --> 00:28:55,677
Rachel Crawcheck.

542
00:28:56,417 --> 00:28:57,647
Therapist.

543
00:28:58,857 --> 00:29:00,269
Do they know about that?

544
00:29:00,287 --> 00:29:03,052
They know everything else
about my private life.

545
00:29:03,827 --> 00:29:06,256
You've been in scrapes,
but never this serious.

546
00:29:06,257 --> 00:29:07,956
Why don't you keep your mouth shut?

547
00:29:07,957 --> 00:29:09,996
Then neither of us would be in trouble.

548
00:29:09,997 --> 00:29:12,547
What does your head of chambers
have to say?

549
00:29:13,367 --> 00:29:15,366
Your press coverage
is all anyone wants to talk about.

550
00:29:15,367 --> 00:29:16,906
What are they gonna do, sack me?

551
00:29:16,907 --> 00:29:18,966
Nobody's sacked a judge since 1830.

552
00:29:19,237 --> 00:29:20,637
But can you win?

553
00:29:21,477 --> 00:29:23,606
I'm not sure what winning would mean.

554
00:29:23,607 --> 00:29:25,516
Having Legover back down, for a start.

555
00:29:25,517 --> 00:29:27,516
As long as Brooklands remains free,

556
00:29:27,517 --> 00:29:29,282
the justice system is tainted.

557
00:29:29,287 --> 00:29:31,507
You cannot force the Attorney General

558
00:29:31,887 --> 00:29:32,956
to appeal the sentence.

559
00:29:32,957 --> 00:29:36,487
No, exactly. Alan Peasmarsh is
part of the political machine.

560
00:29:37,487 --> 00:29:39,856
Law Officers should
be entirely independent,

561
00:29:39,857 --> 00:29:42,096
free to advise the government
according to law,

562
00:29:42,097 --> 00:29:43,274
not party interests.

563
00:29:43,497 --> 00:29:46,166
Lower your sights, John.
Just go after Monty Everard.

564
00:29:46,167 --> 00:29:49,006
No, that's like treating the symptom
and not the cause.

565
00:29:49,007 --> 00:29:52,076
He wouldn't have behaved as he did
without a nod from those weasels.

566
00:29:52,077 --> 00:29:54,447
You're either very brave
or wholly right,

567
00:29:54,847 --> 00:29:56,176
and I don't know which.

568
00:29:56,177 --> 00:29:57,667
Or totally foolish.

569
00:30:00,047 --> 00:30:02,930
Oh, maybe I ought to marry you
and look after you.

570
00:30:14,697 --> 00:30:16,477
They're all ready, Judge.

571
00:30:16,967 --> 00:30:19,196
Did we hear
from the Attorney General yet?

572
00:30:19,197 --> 00:30:21,196
Sir Alan Peasmarsh is in a meeting,

573
00:30:21,197 --> 00:30:23,006
which is likely to go on all day.

574
00:30:23,007 --> 00:30:25,227
Keep trying, Coop, I want a meeting.

575
00:30:26,577 --> 00:30:29,737
Chief Inspector,
after the defendant was charged,

576
00:30:30,407 --> 00:30:32,687
can you tell the court what she said?

577
00:30:33,217 --> 00:30:34,827
She changed her story.

578
00:30:35,117 --> 00:30:38,353
She said it wasn't intruders
who had killed her husband,

579
00:30:38,687 --> 00:30:40,617
it was her following a row.

580
00:30:41,627 --> 00:30:44,907
When did the intruders
first enter the story?

581
00:30:45,697 --> 00:30:48,726
When I interviewed both her and her son
at the house.

582
00:30:48,727 --> 00:30:50,977
Both had exactly similar stories.

583
00:30:51,797 --> 00:30:53,033
Did you believe them?

584
00:30:53,237 --> 00:30:57,120
At first it seemed to make sense,
but we found no sign of burglary.

585
00:30:58,007 --> 00:31:00,667
Did the defendant explain that?

586
00:31:01,137 --> 00:31:03,549
She said Andy had
opened the door to them.

587
00:31:03,977 --> 00:31:07,376
Well, the story came apart
at the station and she confessed.

588
00:31:07,377 --> 00:31:11,067
Hmm, did you establish a motive?

589
00:31:12,257 --> 00:31:14,610
Well, Mr and Mrs Dobbs rowed frequently.

590
00:31:14,757 --> 00:31:18,346
Mostly about her wanting to leave him
and go off with her son.

591
00:31:19,057 --> 00:31:22,366
Mr Dobbs wouldn't agree to a divorce.
He's a devout Catholic.

592
00:31:22,367 --> 00:31:24,797
DEED: Was that a motive,
Chief Inspector?

593
00:31:25,837 --> 00:31:29,707
He couldn't stop her divorcing him
after the statutory separation.

594
00:31:31,107 --> 00:31:33,578
Mrs Dobbs was dependent
on him financially.

595
00:31:34,537 --> 00:31:37,890
Well, that makes for an even less sound
motive for murder.

596
00:31:38,947 --> 00:31:42,077
He held some threat over her
about the boy,

597
00:31:43,117 --> 00:31:46,059
but we couldn't get from the defendant
what it was.

598
00:31:46,117 --> 00:31:50,157
Well, perhaps we'll learn it
during the defence's case.

599
00:31:50,827 --> 00:31:53,256
When Mrs Dobbs changed her story,
what was your reaction?

600
00:31:53,257 --> 00:31:54,517
Did you believe her?

601
00:31:54,757 --> 00:31:57,127
Not at first. She didn't seem the type.

602
00:31:58,537 --> 00:32:00,796
Is there a type
that murders their spouse?

603
00:32:00,797 --> 00:32:01,967
Broadly, there is.

604
00:32:02,207 --> 00:32:04,667
Usually more
emotionally volatile people.

605
00:32:05,177 --> 00:32:07,607
Mrs Dobbs was very demure.

606
00:32:08,177 --> 00:32:11,687
POWELL: Demure. Dedicated.
A close mother.

607
00:32:13,047 --> 00:32:17,027
Yes. Our inquiries suggested
she was too close.

608
00:32:17,647 --> 00:32:18,727
Overly protective.

609
00:32:18,987 --> 00:32:21,726
It's most mothers' instinct
to protect their young.

610
00:32:21,727 --> 00:32:23,837
That's most parents' instinct.

611
00:32:24,227 --> 00:32:26,496
Can a mother, therefore,
be overprotective,

612
00:32:26,497 --> 00:32:28,277
especially in today's climate?

613
00:32:28,697 --> 00:32:31,717
Some witnesses say it was cloying.
He is 16.

614
00:32:32,537 --> 00:32:37,243
You say that as if it's an age where she
should have nothing more to do with him.

615
00:32:38,777 --> 00:32:40,147
Are you a parent?

616
00:32:41,977 --> 00:32:42,977
No.

617
00:32:43,577 --> 00:32:46,016
Then would it be fair to say
that your only contact

618
00:32:46,017 --> 00:32:48,286
with young teenagers
has been at the rougher end?

619
00:32:48,287 --> 00:32:51,067
I have dealt with a lot of kids
in trouble.

620
00:32:51,687 --> 00:32:55,286
Would it be true to say that you
haven't experienced the kind of anxiety

621
00:32:55,287 --> 00:32:57,717
that most parents have
for their children?

622
00:32:58,127 --> 00:33:00,407
Yes, I haven't experienced that.

623
00:33:01,457 --> 00:33:04,222
Then are you in a position to say
how protective

624
00:33:04,367 --> 00:33:06,796
a mother should be
when her son is being attacked?

625
00:33:06,797 --> 00:33:09,347
She bludgeoned the man to death.

626
00:33:10,307 --> 00:33:14,543
This demure little woman turned into
a tiger in defence of her offspring,

627
00:33:15,647 --> 00:33:18,077
who was being attacked
by a savage beast.

628
00:33:19,947 --> 00:33:22,889
You do accept he was,
at that time, a savage beast?

629
00:33:23,517 --> 00:33:25,247
No, I don't.

630
00:33:28,017 --> 00:33:30,547
Did you look into the history
of the victim?

631
00:33:31,427 --> 00:33:32,427
No.

632
00:33:32,657 --> 00:33:35,557
Even though he was killed
while attacking his son?

633
00:33:36,067 --> 00:33:38,244
Well, we only have her word for that.

634
00:33:38,397 --> 00:33:41,280
You clearly didn't examine
the victim's background

635
00:33:41,497 --> 00:33:45,262
other than for clues that might
strengthen the motive for murder.

636
00:33:46,037 --> 00:33:48,406
Had you done so,
his medical history would have shown

637
00:33:48,407 --> 00:33:51,887
that Mr Dobbs was on a high dose
of lithium to stabilise him

638
00:33:53,047 --> 00:33:57,047
and stop his violent and destructive
bouts of melancholia. It's here,

639
00:33:57,317 --> 00:33:58,486
in his medical record.

640
00:33:58,487 --> 00:34:00,605
Are you exhibiting these, Mr Powell?

641
00:34:00,817 --> 00:34:03,956
I will be, my lord, along with an
independent report from a pathologist

642
00:34:03,957 --> 00:34:07,134
that shows there was no
lithium in his blood or organs.

643
00:34:07,497 --> 00:34:08,517
Okay.

644
00:34:08,727 --> 00:34:10,696
This bears out our contention
that Mr Dobbs

645
00:34:10,697 --> 00:34:12,596
hadn't been taking his medication.

646
00:34:12,597 --> 00:34:15,715
As a result of this,
he was prone to violent outbursts

647
00:34:16,767 --> 00:34:19,897
and splenetic bouts
of accusatory paranoia.

648
00:34:22,007 --> 00:34:25,137
In the event of a man
prone to such violent outbursts,

649
00:34:27,017 --> 00:34:28,716
is it not reasonable
that a loving mother

650
00:34:28,717 --> 00:34:30,967
might lash out to protect her son?

651
00:34:31,987 --> 00:34:34,767
Yes, when put like that.

652
00:34:37,087 --> 00:34:39,970
I'd like to speak
to the Attorney General, please.

653
00:34:42,127 --> 00:34:44,467
That's what you keep telling my clerk.

654
00:34:44,897 --> 00:34:47,666
I do intend to speak to Sir Alan
and if that doesn't happen soon,

655
00:34:47,667 --> 00:34:50,197
I'm going to come
and camp outside his door.

656
00:34:51,567 --> 00:34:53,177
He knows what it's about.

657
00:34:54,537 --> 00:34:56,027
(SLAMS PHONE DOWN)

658
00:34:56,477 --> 00:34:58,057
No luck, Judge?

659
00:34:58,617 --> 00:34:59,746
How long can he avoid me?

660
00:34:59,747 --> 00:35:01,737
(SCOFFS) A lot longer than this.

661
00:35:02,847 --> 00:35:05,495
There's a priest
trying to contact you, Judge.

662
00:35:05,887 --> 00:35:07,467
A Father Cattle.

663
00:35:08,657 --> 00:35:10,363
Did he say what it was about?

664
00:35:10,427 --> 00:35:11,767
He wouldn't say.

665
00:35:12,057 --> 00:35:15,469
Perhaps the Lord Chancellor
sent him to hear my confession.

666
00:35:16,497 --> 00:35:18,596
Is your brother
still pursuing judicial review?

667
00:35:18,597 --> 00:35:20,596
Oh, we're seeing Mrs Mills this evening.

668
00:35:20,597 --> 00:35:21,597
Hmm.

669
00:35:22,537 --> 00:35:26,667
Well, Father Cattle
better say what it is he wants.

670
00:35:27,977 --> 00:35:30,087
You haven't forgotten Dr Crawcheck?

671
00:35:30,447 --> 00:35:31,447
No.

672
00:35:33,047 --> 00:35:35,157
Tell me about your day, Sir John.

673
00:35:35,687 --> 00:35:38,393
- What I do?
- Is that how you define your day?

674
00:35:40,217 --> 00:35:42,587
Well, the law takes up most of my time.

675
00:35:43,457 --> 00:35:45,627
Do you wake thinking about the law?

676
00:35:46,297 --> 00:35:47,467
Sometimes.

677
00:35:47,857 --> 00:35:49,975
Reviewing a decision I may have made

678
00:35:49,997 --> 00:35:51,897
or have still to make.

679
00:35:53,467 --> 00:35:56,427
Sometimes worrying about something
in the past.

680
00:35:57,707 --> 00:35:59,317
Do you do that often?

681
00:35:59,937 --> 00:36:02,606
Wake up worrying about mistakes
I may have made?

682
00:36:02,607 --> 00:36:03,977
No, not often.

683
00:36:04,647 --> 00:36:07,236
Is that because
you don't often get it wrong?

684
00:36:07,547 --> 00:36:09,187
Well, I do try not to.

685
00:36:09,987 --> 00:36:12,157
That wasn't what I asked, Sir John.

686
00:36:13,657 --> 00:36:17,377
Well, I sometimes get things wrong,
of course.

687
00:36:24,397 --> 00:36:27,221
Making public my row
with government law officers

688
00:36:27,297 --> 00:36:29,517
may have been a mistake.

689
00:36:31,207 --> 00:36:32,736
Are you going to do anything about it?

690
00:36:32,737 --> 00:36:35,443
What's the purpose
of this line of questioning?

691
00:36:36,177 --> 00:36:39,146
I'm trying to establish that if,
when you recognise a mistake,

692
00:36:39,147 --> 00:36:40,795
you're able to put it right.

693
00:36:40,947 --> 00:36:41,947
Ah.

694
00:36:42,847 --> 00:36:44,217
Well, you see,

695
00:36:45,317 --> 00:36:47,017
the problem with the law

696
00:36:47,317 --> 00:36:50,670
is that you often don't realise
that you've made a mistake

697
00:36:50,827 --> 00:36:53,769
until someone on the Appellate Bench
puts it right.

698
00:36:55,227 --> 00:36:56,777
What about elsewhere?

699
00:37:01,167 --> 00:37:04,647
I don't wake up sweating
about mistakes I may have made.

700
00:37:05,637 --> 00:37:07,006
Would you recognise them?

701
00:37:07,007 --> 00:37:08,587
I don't have regrets.

702
00:37:10,477 --> 00:37:13,242
Can you tell me
about your sexual relationships?

703
00:37:13,547 --> 00:37:15,547
Can you be a little more specific?

704
00:37:15,887 --> 00:37:18,416
Do you ever think about
the women you've slept with?

705
00:37:18,417 --> 00:37:19,417
Yes.

706
00:37:19,817 --> 00:37:22,170
Especially if I'm still in bed
with them.

707
00:37:23,527 --> 00:37:25,457
The next day, the day after?

708
00:37:27,157 --> 00:37:29,347
They're not my preoccupation.

709
00:37:29,727 --> 00:37:31,597
Could you do without women?

710
00:37:32,267 --> 00:37:33,766
I thought the idea of this

711
00:37:33,767 --> 00:37:36,736
was to get me into the sort of shape
where I could successfully

712
00:37:36,737 --> 00:37:37,847
purSue One WOman.

713
00:37:38,107 --> 00:37:40,460
Therapy doesn't work like that,
Sir John.

714
00:37:42,807 --> 00:37:45,455
I'm scared the Judge
isn't gonna survive this.

715
00:37:45,477 --> 00:37:47,576
He's been in other scrapes with the LCD.

716
00:37:47,577 --> 00:37:49,507
Nothing so serious.

717
00:37:50,587 --> 00:37:52,777
What's being said around the Court?

718
00:37:53,387 --> 00:37:55,086
Well, it's thought he's on his way out.

719
00:37:55,087 --> 00:37:57,126
That's not realistic.
He's a High Court judge.

720
00:37:57,127 --> 00:37:59,757
No one has ever spoken out like that.

721
00:38:01,527 --> 00:38:03,626
The trouble is the more you tell him
not to do something,

722
00:38:03,627 --> 00:38:05,467
the more he tends to do it.

723
00:38:07,267 --> 00:38:09,606
He was right to say what he said,
Mrs Mills.

724
00:38:09,607 --> 00:38:11,636
It doesn't help him, though, does it?

725
00:38:11,637 --> 00:38:13,097
(INTERCOM BEEPING)

726
00:38:13,377 --> 00:38:14,730
- Yes?
- MAN: <i>Mrs Mills?

727
00:38:14,777 --> 00:38:16,346
<i>Mrs Cooper</i>'s brother is here.

728
00:38:16,347 --> 00:38:17,637
Show him up.

729
00:38:19,017 --> 00:38:20,665
Will the Judge be all right?

730
00:38:23,647 --> 00:38:26,000
What is your earliest memory as a child?

731
00:38:27,317 --> 00:38:29,127
Um, at the zoo.

732
00:38:30,387 --> 00:38:32,577
My dad giving a monkey a toffee.

733
00:38:32,957 --> 00:38:35,605
The monkey unwrapped the toffee,
ate the paper

734
00:38:36,027 --> 00:38:37,667
and threw the toffee away.

735
00:38:37,967 --> 00:38:39,167
I was two.

736
00:38:39,397 --> 00:38:40,917
Is that a happy memory?

737
00:38:41,197 --> 00:38:42,903
I think of it with affection.

738
00:38:43,907 --> 00:38:45,206
You have brothers and sisters?

739
00:38:45,207 --> 00:38:46,443
Yes, an older sister.

740
00:38:47,237 --> 00:38:49,817
My mother adopted me
after she miscarried

741
00:38:50,247 --> 00:38:52,130
and couldn't have more children.

742
00:38:53,217 --> 00:38:56,046
Why did it take you almost an hour
to tell me that?

743
00:38:56,047 --> 00:38:57,307
You didn't ask me.

744
00:38:59,117 --> 00:39:00,927
Does it disturb you, Sir John?

745
00:39:02,857 --> 00:39:05,757
I had a very happy early childhood.

746
00:39:07,127 --> 00:39:09,757
I didn't find out I was adopted
until my 50s.

747
00:39:11,027 --> 00:39:14,466
Do you know the circumstances of
your separation from your blood mother?

748
00:39:14,467 --> 00:39:15,987
No, didn't need to.

749
00:39:16,737 --> 00:39:18,679
My adoptive mother was my mother,

750
00:39:18,977 --> 00:39:20,757
as far as I was concerned.

751
00:39:21,707 --> 00:39:22,825
Is she still alive?

752
00:39:22,847 --> 00:39:25,307
No, she committed suicide when I was 10.

753
00:39:49,707 --> 00:39:51,437
I think this is painful.

754
00:39:59,247 --> 00:40:00,917
(INTERCOM BEEPING)

755
00:40:03,887 --> 00:40:06,005
We have to leave it there, Sir John.

756
00:40:06,087 --> 00:40:07,676
Next week at the same time?

757
00:40:10,187 --> 00:40:11,587
Why not?

758
00:40:11,857 --> 00:40:13,667
It's entirely up to you.

759
00:40:16,067 --> 00:40:18,147
Yes, I would like to come again.

760
00:40:25,167 --> 00:40:26,427
You're alone?

761
00:40:27,107 --> 00:40:30,107
Just when I was beginning
to think my life was over,

762
00:40:31,047 --> 00:40:33,776
I'm gonna have to spend
the whole evening here on my own

763
00:40:33,777 --> 00:40:35,737
and then you walk in.

764
00:40:39,157 --> 00:40:40,746
I saw Coop and her brother.

765
00:40:42,487 --> 00:40:44,586
- Is he going ahead?
- We talked about you.

766
00:40:44,587 --> 00:40:46,117
- How you'll survive.
- Ah.

767
00:40:46,897 --> 00:40:50,896
Do you remember that animal rights
protestor who went on hunger strike?

768
00:40:50,897 --> 00:40:53,147
Well, going on hunger strike is novel.

769
00:40:53,537 --> 00:40:55,936
You'll be the thinnest High Court judge
in history.

770
00:40:55,937 --> 00:40:57,747
Charlie said to me then,

771
00:40:58,907 --> 00:41:01,367
"If the law can't deliver justice
for him,

772
00:41:02,177 --> 00:41:05,346
"then I don't see any point
in carrying on with the law."

773
00:41:05,347 --> 00:41:06,616
I'm beginning to know how she feels.

774
00:41:06,617 --> 00:41:09,077
Judicial suicide won't help.
They'll win.

775
00:41:10,147 --> 00:41:11,286
You staying for supper?

776
00:41:11,287 --> 00:41:12,397
(CHUCKLING)

777
00:41:12,617 --> 00:41:14,547
Will you give up this fight?

778
00:41:14,887 --> 00:41:16,377
Is that your price?

779
00:41:16,657 --> 00:41:18,775
You may have the media on your side,

780
00:41:18,827 --> 00:41:20,437
but can you rely on them?

781
00:41:20,727 --> 00:41:22,026
Public opinion's on my side.

782
00:41:22,027 --> 00:41:23,897
That's even more fickle, John.

783
00:41:24,427 --> 00:41:26,297
All I need is a bit of luck.

784
00:41:26,867 --> 00:41:28,196
You've never fought this way before.

785
00:41:28,197 --> 00:41:30,736
Every other tilt at them has been
with the sharpness of your intellect.

786
00:41:30,737 --> 00:41:33,796
- Has it suddenly become dull?
- This is about pride.

787
00:41:34,377 --> 00:41:36,957
Now, I don't care if you win or lose.

788
00:41:37,407 --> 00:41:39,247
I do care about you.

789
00:41:40,047 --> 00:41:41,627
Stop this.

790
00:41:45,617 --> 00:41:47,017
Let's get married.

791
00:41:52,697 --> 00:41:54,867
- You don't mean that.
- Yes, I do.

792
00:41:57,167 --> 00:41:58,266
You're my dream, John.

793
00:41:58,267 --> 00:42:02,856
Ever since I was a young woman and you
helped my father when he was in trouble.

794
00:42:04,767 --> 00:42:07,062
You were everything a woman could want.

795
00:42:08,237 --> 00:42:09,546
I think about you every day.

796
00:42:09,547 --> 00:42:11,376
I was too scared to let you in
too close

797
00:42:11,377 --> 00:42:15,554
in case you overwhelmed me, but all
I ever wanted was to be overwhelmed.

798
00:42:19,187 --> 00:42:21,656
If I was half as smart
as you seem to think I am,

799
00:42:21,657 --> 00:42:23,467
I would have realised that.

800
00:42:24,187 --> 00:42:28,070
I've been too busy telling myself
I didn't care, I didn't want you.

801
00:42:28,827 --> 00:42:32,536
I've always known you were a womaniser,
and perhaps you always will be.

802
00:42:32,537 --> 00:42:34,066
I can pretend you love only me.

803
00:42:34,067 --> 00:42:37,303
I do love only you.
Why do you think I'm having therapy?

804
00:42:37,367 --> 00:42:40,250
- So I can commit to one person.
- So is that yes?

805
00:42:42,947 --> 00:42:44,677
You'll stop fighting them?

806
00:42:46,347 --> 00:42:49,046
- We'll get married?
- Does one depend upon the other?

807
00:42:49,047 --> 00:42:52,812
- I mean, are they mutually exclusive?
- Yes, I'm making them so.

808
00:42:55,117 --> 00:42:57,577
To see injustice like that
and just let it

809
00:42:58,927 --> 00:43:00,633
slide away without a whimper,

810
00:43:00,657 --> 00:43:02,626
it's more than I could stand.
It's more than you could stand.

811
00:43:02,627 --> 00:43:04,216
But there are other fights.

812
00:43:04,267 --> 00:43:08,091
Get to the appeal court. There you can
change what governments do.

813
00:43:08,397 --> 00:43:10,917
Don't ask me to back away from this, Jo.

814
00:43:17,307 --> 00:43:20,207
You stupid, stupid man.

815
00:43:29,857 --> 00:43:33,517
Dr Graceman, you carried out
a post mortem on Alexander Dobbs.

816
00:43:34,427 --> 00:43:37,545
Can you tell the court
the extent of that examination?

817
00:43:37,667 --> 00:43:39,477
It was a full post mortem.

818
00:43:39,967 --> 00:43:42,107
All the major organs, the brain,

819
00:43:42,507 --> 00:43:44,697
tissue and blood were examined.

820
00:43:45,367 --> 00:43:48,177
Was there anything particular
in your findings?

821
00:43:49,147 --> 00:43:51,500
The right side of the skull was crushed,

822
00:43:51,607 --> 00:43:54,784
suggesting a blow from behind
by a right<i>-</i>handed person.

823
00:43:56,147 --> 00:43:58,867
Splintered bone had
severed the carotid artery.

824
00:44:00,017 --> 00:44:03,737
The force of the blow drove a splinter
deep into the brain stem.

825
00:44:04,327 --> 00:44:06,197
Death was instantaneous.

826
00:44:06,927 --> 00:44:09,163
Did the second, third and fourth blows

827
00:44:09,267 --> 00:44:11,856
indicate the manner
in which he was attacked?

828
00:44:11,867 --> 00:44:14,573
Yes. It was savage,
like someone going berserk.

829
00:44:16,307 --> 00:44:18,857
Did you analyse the blood, Doctor?

830
00:44:19,777 --> 00:44:20,827
I did.

831
00:44:21,277 --> 00:44:24,906
In the light of the statement given
to the police by the defendant,

832
00:44:24,907 --> 00:44:26,517
I examined the blood

833
00:44:27,047 --> 00:44:30,007
and had a blood scientist
examine it as well.

834
00:44:30,487 --> 00:44:33,487
And what was particular
to the defendant's statement

835
00:44:33,787 --> 00:44:35,427
that made you do this?

836
00:44:35,727 --> 00:44:39,207
She told the police her husband
had been attacking first her

837
00:44:39,827 --> 00:44:40,967
then her son.

838
00:44:41,227 --> 00:44:43,169
It was at that point she hit him.

839
00:44:43,467 --> 00:44:47,597
And did you find any inconsistency
in her story?

840
00:44:48,297 --> 00:44:49,557
I did.

841
00:44:49,807 --> 00:44:52,219
If the victim had been attacking
first one

842
00:44:52,377 --> 00:44:54,495
then the other member of his family,

843
00:44:54,607 --> 00:44:58,490
there would almost certainly
have been free adrenaline in his blood

844
00:44:58,577 --> 00:45:00,827
from his stimulated adrenal glands.

845
00:45:01,577 --> 00:45:02,977
We found none.

846
00:45:03,687 --> 00:45:06,407
Does the blood indicate
the state of the body

847
00:45:06,857 --> 00:45:08,967
immediately prior to death?

848
00:45:09,327 --> 00:45:13,396
Generally, it indicates whether a person
had been drinking, taking drugs,

849
00:45:13,397 --> 00:45:15,177
eating agreeable food.

850
00:45:15,497 --> 00:45:18,837
Were any of these
indicators present in Mr Dobbs?

851
00:45:19,537 --> 00:45:23,257
The red platelets indicated
he was deeply relaxed,

852
00:45:24,207 --> 00:45:27,207
possibly listening to something
like Mozart or Bach.

853
00:45:27,537 --> 00:45:29,997
Would Bach make a difference
to the blood?

854
00:45:30,577 --> 00:45:33,887
Indeed, my lord.
Something like the cantatas

855
00:45:34,417 --> 00:45:37,417
beneficially affects
the alpha rhythms of the brain.

856
00:45:37,687 --> 00:45:40,817
Heavy rock, like Black Sabbath,
stimulates the brain,

857
00:45:41,357 --> 00:45:43,652
causing the adrenal glands to overwork.

858
00:45:43,987 --> 00:45:45,635
I'd better tell my daughter.

859
00:45:45,797 --> 00:45:48,226
Does your daughter listen
to Black Sabbath, my lord?

860
00:45:48,227 --> 00:45:49,227
I'd be surprised.

861
00:45:49,297 --> 00:45:50,297
(LAUGHING)

862
00:45:50,427 --> 00:45:51,857
She's a big fan.

863
00:45:52,437 --> 00:45:54,107
One of our shared pleasures.

864
00:45:54,437 --> 00:45:55,437
(JURY CHUCKLING)

865
00:45:58,907 --> 00:46:01,597
Let us get this right, Dr Graceman.

866
00:46:02,407 --> 00:46:05,537
The assailant would've crept up
on the victim,

867
00:46:06,147 --> 00:46:09,046
while he was asleep,
and smashed him over the head.

868
00:46:09,047 --> 00:46:11,546
My lord, my learned friend
is leading the witness.

869
00:46:11,547 --> 00:46:13,686
Yes, he's going in for the kill,
Mr Powell.

870
00:46:13,687 --> 00:46:16,335
Possibly he's been
listening to Black Sabbath.

871
00:46:16,587 --> 00:46:20,187
I will try to
re<i>-</i>educate my musical taste, sir.

872
00:46:20,797 --> 00:46:22,680
Yes, and rephrase your question.

873
00:46:22,927 --> 00:46:25,067
Well, I withdraw the question.

874
00:46:28,497 --> 00:46:29,697
Doctor.

875
00:46:30,537 --> 00:46:34,420
You said, "If the victim had been
on his feet, attacking first one,

876
00:46:34,507 --> 00:46:36,176
"then the other member of his family,

877
00:46:36,177 --> 00:46:39,825
"there would almost certainly be
free adrenaline in his blood."

878
00:46:41,117 --> 00:46:43,137
Why "almost certainly", Doctor?

879
00:46:43,517 --> 00:46:45,537
Why can't you be more definite?

880
00:46:45,887 --> 00:46:49,456
Why not, "There would certainly be
free adrenaline in his blood"?

881
00:46:49,457 --> 00:46:52,557
Because one can never be absolute.

882
00:46:55,297 --> 00:46:57,650
In other words,
it doesn't always happen.

883
00:46:57,967 --> 00:47:01,566
Nor can one be absolute about adrenaline
entering the bloodstream,

884
00:47:01,567 --> 00:47:03,057
as you describe.

885
00:47:05,567 --> 00:47:07,177
Is it not true that lithium

886
00:47:07,507 --> 00:47:09,467
suppresses adrenal response?

887
00:47:10,807 --> 00:47:14,160
So, there wouldn't necessarily be
adrenaline in the blood.

888
00:47:14,517 --> 00:47:15,977
Yes, it can do.

889
00:47:16,387 --> 00:47:17,937
Thank you, Doctor.

890
00:47:19,657 --> 00:47:21,497
DEED: Mr Mason Allen.

891
00:47:24,857 --> 00:47:27,096
Was there lithium in the victim's blood?

892
00:47:27,097 --> 00:47:28,437
None was found.

893
00:47:31,027 --> 00:47:32,367
Thank you, Doctor.

894
00:47:33,167 --> 00:47:35,050
Dr Graceman was my last witness.

895
00:47:35,237 --> 00:47:38,179
And that concludes
the case for the Crown, my lord.

896
00:47:41,607 --> 00:47:44,067
The priest, Father Cattle,

897
00:47:44,507 --> 00:47:46,997
is most insistent
about seeing you, Judge.

898
00:47:47,547 --> 00:47:49,016
And he won't say what it's about?

899
00:47:49,017 --> 00:47:51,886
He said having read your bold
statements in the newspaper

900
00:47:51,887 --> 00:47:53,197
made him bold.

901
00:47:53,957 --> 00:47:56,075
Perhaps it's not my soul he's after.

902
00:47:56,357 --> 00:47:57,986
All right, Coop, make an appointment.

903
00:47:57,987 --> 00:47:59,796
Well, he's in the building now.

904
00:47:59,797 --> 00:48:02,974
Well, I could give him 10 minutes,
as I've risen early.

905
00:48:03,397 --> 00:48:04,477
Thanks.

906
00:48:11,237 --> 00:48:14,367
These entries make very
interesting reading, Father.

907
00:48:17,977 --> 00:48:20,437
What were you doing
with Mr Dobbs' diary?

908
00:48:21,017 --> 00:48:23,507
He wanted me to have it for safekeeping.

909
00:48:24,117 --> 00:48:26,588
- What did he mean by that?
- I don't know.

910
00:48:27,557 --> 00:48:29,426
I'm not at liberty
to tell what he told me

911
00:48:29,427 --> 00:48:31,604
in confession about his wife and son.

912
00:48:33,397 --> 00:48:35,750
I have no means of knowing if it's true.

913
00:48:36,627 --> 00:48:38,407
He was a troubled soul.

914
00:48:39,337 --> 00:48:43,066
I don't know if I can use it now,
the prosecution's closed its case.

915
00:48:43,067 --> 00:48:44,557
I think it's too late.

916
00:48:44,867 --> 00:48:47,976
I did wrestle with my conscience for
a long while about going to the police.

917
00:48:47,977 --> 00:48:50,346
Yes, I think perhaps
you should have done.

918
00:48:50,347 --> 00:48:53,347
Well, I was trying to reconcile
the need for justice

919
00:48:53,777 --> 00:48:56,946
with the question of confidence
I owe to Mr Dobbs even now.

920
00:48:56,947 --> 00:48:57,997
(SIGHS)

921
00:48:58,817 --> 00:49:02,277
If what Mr Dobbs
has written in his diary is true,

922
00:49:03,357 --> 00:49:06,377
it provides a motive
for Mrs Dobbs killing him.

923
00:49:14,897 --> 00:49:18,897
I'll leave it to your conscience
how best to use the diary, Sir John.

924
00:49:20,307 --> 00:49:22,484
I'll pray for you to do what's right.

925
00:49:45,297 --> 00:49:47,047
POWELL: Mrs Dobbs,

926
00:49:47,367 --> 00:49:50,367
was your relationship
with your husband a happy one?

927
00:49:51,837 --> 00:49:52,917
No.

928
00:49:53,907 --> 00:49:56,277
I don't know why I married him, really.

929
00:49:56,777 --> 00:49:58,447
No, that's not true.

930
00:49:59,277 --> 00:50:00,357
I do.

931
00:50:01,847 --> 00:50:03,597
It was for support.

932
00:50:03,947 --> 00:50:05,497
Mutual support.

933
00:50:06,557 --> 00:50:10,017
I'd recently lost my mother,
who I'd nursed since I was 15.

934
00:50:11,387 --> 00:50:12,907
She'd had a stroke

935
00:50:14,297 --> 00:50:16,877
and begged me not to let her
go into a home.

936
00:50:19,537 --> 00:50:22,637
Alec had lost his mother
around the same time.

937
00:50:23,837 --> 00:50:25,797
She'd also had a stroke.

938
00:50:26,237 --> 00:50:28,927
We met at
the stroke victims support group.

939
00:50:30,207 --> 00:50:32,855
Did you feel anything
for the man you married?

940
00:50:33,177 --> 00:50:34,547
I admired him

941
00:50:35,247 --> 00:50:37,707
for supporting his mother
the way he did.

942
00:50:40,087 --> 00:50:42,547
Alec had wonderful qualities,

943
00:50:42,987 --> 00:50:44,856
which I thought
he'd bring to our marriage,

944
00:50:44,857 --> 00:50:46,667
and, at first, he did.

945
00:50:47,427 --> 00:50:49,097
And when I got pregnant,

946
00:50:51,067 --> 00:50:53,377
I thought they'd develop into love.

947
00:50:54,937 --> 00:50:58,567
But instead, he got more
and more demanding.

948
00:51:00,207 --> 00:51:02,377
He accused me of having an affair

949
00:51:04,577 --> 00:51:07,067
and saying Andy wasn't his child.

950
00:51:09,717 --> 00:51:12,087
Were you able to convince him he was?

951
00:51:12,517 --> 00:51:16,117
Only after DNA tests showed that
he was the father.

952
00:51:18,757 --> 00:51:20,597
Then he said

953
00:51:21,327 --> 00:51:24,607
I'd slept with the doctor
and got him to lie.

954
00:51:27,067 --> 00:51:28,497
Soon after that,

955
00:51:29,367 --> 00:51:32,057
Alec agreed to have
psychiatric treatment.

956
00:51:32,567 --> 00:51:34,177
Did this help at all?

957
00:51:35,707 --> 00:51:37,257
He quietened down,

958
00:51:38,577 --> 00:51:40,447
was less accusatory.

959
00:51:42,547 --> 00:51:44,900
Then he started finding fault with Andy.

960
00:51:45,417 --> 00:51:47,407
What sort of fault, Mrs Dobbs?

961
00:51:50,027 --> 00:51:51,257
He said...

962
00:51:52,427 --> 00:51:55,237
He said his ears stuck out too much,

963
00:51:56,067 --> 00:51:57,617
that they were too low.

964
00:51:57,897 --> 00:51:59,827
His eyes were too wide apart,

965
00:52:00,197 --> 00:52:01,537
he was too small.

966
00:52:01,837 --> 00:52:03,647
And then when Andy grew older,

967
00:52:03,967 --> 00:52:06,236
he was too slow,
he wasn't strong enough.

968
00:52:06,237 --> 00:52:08,757
Andy didn't stand a chance.

969
00:52:09,177 --> 00:52:11,766
Did the psychiatrist
say anything about this?

970
00:52:14,417 --> 00:52:15,727
He told me

971
00:52:16,747 --> 00:52:19,967
perfectionism was a side effect
of his medication.

972
00:52:22,257 --> 00:52:23,257
(SIGHS DEEPLY)

973
00:52:23,387 --> 00:52:24,757
And coldness

974
00:52:25,827 --> 00:52:27,437
and remoteness.

975
00:52:28,627 --> 00:52:31,804
Did you and your husband enjoy
a physical relationship?

976
00:52:35,637 --> 00:52:36,717
No.

977
00:52:40,537 --> 00:52:43,467
Impotence was another side effect.

978
00:52:45,607 --> 00:52:47,797
Did he seek medical help for that?

979
00:52:50,917 --> 00:52:52,347
He refused to.

980
00:52:53,987 --> 00:52:56,477
He said the problem was mine.

981
00:52:58,557 --> 00:53:00,577
I was sexually uninteresting.

982
00:53:02,427 --> 00:53:03,687
That I was frigid.

983
00:53:03,927 --> 00:53:05,677
Were you, Mrs Dobbs?

984
00:53:08,037 --> 00:53:09,737
I've no way of telling.

985
00:53:12,507 --> 00:53:14,207
I'd only known Alec.

986
00:53:14,537 --> 00:53:18,243
You hadn't been with another man
before or during your marriage?

987
00:53:19,107 --> 00:53:21,127
No, I'm afraid I haven't.

988
00:53:21,517 --> 00:53:22,517
Mrs Dobbs,

989
00:53:22,747 --> 00:53:25,546
such fidelity is something for which
we might admire you,

990
00:53:25,547 --> 00:53:27,157
rather than judge you.

991
00:53:28,257 --> 00:53:29,257
Mrs Dobbs,

992
00:53:29,387 --> 00:53:31,026
would you take a look
at this medical record?

993
00:53:31,027 --> 00:53:33,107
Tell the court if you recognise it.

994
00:53:39,837 --> 00:53:41,417
Yes, this is

995
00:53:41,697 --> 00:53:44,657
my husband's psychiatric record.

996
00:53:45,267 --> 00:53:49,091
You can see Dr Jackman's signature
on the medication instructions.

997
00:53:49,137 --> 00:53:51,747
Did Dr Jackman, at any time,
warn you that

998
00:53:52,307 --> 00:53:55,346
your husband might react in this way
to his medication?

999
00:53:55,347 --> 00:53:58,187
When I complained to him
about what it was doing,

1000
00:54:00,257 --> 00:54:01,367
he said,

1001
00:54:01,857 --> 00:54:06,397
would I sooner have a paranoiac
accusing me of things I hadn't done?

1002
00:54:07,097 --> 00:54:08,803
He didn't offer to change it?

1003
00:54:10,727 --> 00:54:11,727
(WHISPERING) No.

1004
00:54:13,397 --> 00:54:14,827
See, Alec wasn't

1005
00:54:15,337 --> 00:54:17,767
physically violent all the while.

1006
00:54:19,507 --> 00:54:20,967
His comments were.

1007
00:54:22,077 --> 00:54:24,707
These, he said, were for my own good.

1008
00:54:28,577 --> 00:54:31,327
His mother was his ideal.

1009
00:54:32,387 --> 00:54:36,256
He said I fell a million miles
short of her and that he should never

1010
00:54:36,257 --> 00:54:38,717
have brought me into her sacred home.

1011
00:54:39,257 --> 00:54:43,266
Mrs Dobbs, you were a relatively young
and healthy woman. Why didn't you leave?

1012
00:54:43,267 --> 00:54:44,317
(SIGHS)

1013
00:54:44,827 --> 00:54:47,017
Because I felt sorry for Alec.

1014
00:54:48,137 --> 00:54:49,447
(CRYING)

1015
00:54:50,037 --> 00:54:52,437
I thought I could help him change.

1016
00:54:53,507 --> 00:54:55,566
Did he show any signs of improving?

1017
00:54:55,877 --> 00:54:56,877
No.

1018
00:54:57,347 --> 00:54:58,607
He got worse.

1019
00:54:59,777 --> 00:55:01,507
Yes. Mrs Dobbs,

1020
00:55:02,817 --> 00:55:08,037
on November the 8th, last year,

1021
00:55:10,227 --> 00:55:13,097
did you tell your sister<i>-</i>in<i>-</i>law,
Madeline Carole,

1022
00:55:13,597 --> 00:55:16,480
that you'd do anything
to get rid of your husband?

1023
00:55:17,197 --> 00:55:18,197
No.

1024
00:55:19,467 --> 00:55:20,577
I didn't.

1025
00:55:21,467 --> 00:55:22,867
(POWELL WHISPERING)

1026
00:55:23,137 --> 00:55:24,366
I don't know what they're talking about.

1027
00:55:24,367 --> 00:55:25,827
DEED: On December

1028
00:55:27,437 --> 00:55:28,547
the 10th,

1029
00:55:29,537 --> 00:55:30,890
did you tell Mrs Carole

1030
00:55:30,947 --> 00:55:34,359
that you were going
to force your husband to sell the house

1031
00:55:34,477 --> 00:55:37,419
- so that you could go away with Andy...
- My lord,

1032
00:55:37,717 --> 00:55:40,587
while we always appreciate
your helpful comments,

1033
00:55:41,057 --> 00:55:45,187
I am concerned to know if
Madeline Carole is somehow being called

1034
00:55:45,957 --> 00:55:48,237
now the prosecution's case is closed.

1035
00:55:51,197 --> 00:55:52,496
Unless you want to call her?

1036
00:55:52,497 --> 00:55:53,727
(CHUCKLES)

1037
00:55:55,697 --> 00:55:56,867
Go on.

1038
00:55:59,107 --> 00:56:00,307
Mrs Dobbs,

1039
00:56:01,777 --> 00:56:03,997
did you, in fact, seek a divorce?

1040
00:56:05,507 --> 00:56:06,907
Yeah, finally...

1041
00:56:08,517 --> 00:56:11,237
Finally, I had to, for Andy's sake.

1042
00:56:12,547 --> 00:56:15,547
And then Alec said he would
kill himself if we left.

1043
00:56:15,787 --> 00:56:19,493
That's a different picture from that
painted by the prosecution.

1044
00:56:19,897 --> 00:56:22,507
Was your husband
different with other people?

1045
00:56:23,467 --> 00:56:24,697
Entirely.

1046
00:56:25,397 --> 00:56:27,567
Oh, he was a bit dull,

1047
00:56:29,267 --> 00:56:30,620
but perfectly pleasant.

1048
00:56:30,767 --> 00:56:34,197
What triggered his attack
on that fatal Sunday afternoon?

1049
00:56:35,737 --> 00:56:36,997
Um, we'd...

1050
00:56:38,377 --> 00:56:40,277
We'd been to morning mass,

1051
00:56:40,677 --> 00:56:42,607
and back at the house, he...

1052
00:56:43,487 --> 00:56:46,527
He accused me of trying to pick up
a man in church.

1053
00:56:47,057 --> 00:56:48,227
Were you?

1054
00:56:48,717 --> 00:56:49,717
No.

1055
00:56:50,587 --> 00:56:51,727
(SNIFFLES)

1056
00:56:52,227 --> 00:56:53,627
No, he kept...

1057
00:56:54,057 --> 00:56:56,247
He kept telling me I was vile

1058
00:56:56,697 --> 00:56:58,657
and Andy was the son of Satan,

1059
00:56:59,397 --> 00:57:02,515
and he would call Father Cattle
to have him exorcised.

1060
00:57:05,167 --> 00:57:07,857
I locked Andy in my room with me,

1061
00:57:09,577 --> 00:57:13,557
hoping that Alec would just calm down.

1062
00:57:16,317 --> 00:57:18,337
Hoping his mania would pass.

1063
00:57:18,917 --> 00:57:21,623
And when I came down
two hours later, he was...

1064
00:57:21,787 --> 00:57:23,657
He was waiting for me.

1065
00:57:25,087 --> 00:57:26,957
He seized me by the throat,

1066
00:57:27,427 --> 00:57:28,797
hissing at me.

1067
00:57:29,597 --> 00:57:30,967
I was the devil.

1068
00:57:31,227 --> 00:57:33,580
That's when Andy came at him
and hit him.

1069
00:57:33,867 --> 00:57:35,985
I thought you said that you hit him.

1070
00:57:35,997 --> 00:57:36,997
Yes.

1071
00:57:38,067 --> 00:57:40,677
I did, with the baseball bat.

1072
00:57:41,437 --> 00:57:43,790
Andy hit him on the back with his fists,

1073
00:57:44,177 --> 00:57:45,957
and only his fists.

1074
00:57:47,917 --> 00:57:49,907
(WHIMPERING)

1075
00:57:50,987 --> 00:57:53,617
Alec turned on him and seized him.

1076
00:57:54,587 --> 00:57:56,577
Where did he have hold of him?

1077
00:57:57,257 --> 00:57:59,127
(SOBBING) Round the throat.

1078
00:58:00,597 --> 00:58:03,317
He was...
All I could hear was him hissing.

1079
00:58:05,527 --> 00:58:07,047
"Son of a serpentl"

1080
00:58:08,237 --> 00:58:10,847
Shaking him. Shaking him violently.

1081
00:58:11,967 --> 00:58:14,320
I tried to break his grip, but he was...

1082
00:58:14,577 --> 00:58:17,387
He was locked tight, like a dog bite.

1083
00:58:17,877 --> 00:58:20,172
And then Andy started going bright red,

1084
00:58:20,377 --> 00:58:22,848
and I grabbed a baseball bat
and I hit himl

1085
00:58:24,217 --> 00:58:28,159
And it just didn't seem to make much
difference, so I hit him again.

1086
00:58:30,857 --> 00:58:32,027
(SOBBING)

1087
00:58:39,937 --> 00:58:41,277
And he was...

1088
00:58:41,737 --> 00:58:43,077
He was still.

1089
00:58:45,967 --> 00:58:47,667
He was just still.

1090
00:58:50,947 --> 00:58:52,147
I'm sorry.

1091
00:59:02,187 --> 00:59:04,587
That poor woman. How she stood by him.

1092
00:59:06,027 --> 00:59:08,487
- You buy her story?
- Don't you?

1093
00:59:09,097 --> 00:59:10,686
I saw the dead man's diary.

1094
00:59:10,827 --> 00:59:12,937
Yeah, but he was paranoid, Judge.

1095
00:59:15,597 --> 00:59:18,936
Are we anywhere with my meeting
with the Attorney General?

1096
00:59:18,937 --> 00:59:22,349
He won't meet with you, Judge.
Well, not willingly, anyway.

1097
00:59:25,707 --> 00:59:27,377
Then I'll doorstep him.

1098
00:59:32,547 --> 00:59:33,947
I'll enjoy this.

1099
00:59:37,657 --> 00:59:39,896
You are on the Attorney's list of calls,
Sir John.

1100
00:59:39,897 --> 00:59:41,296
He will get back to you.

1101
00:59:41,297 --> 00:59:43,826
It has to be within
a meaningful time frame.

1102
00:59:43,827 --> 00:59:46,196
Sir Alan does understand
the urgency, sir.

1103
00:59:46,197 --> 00:59:48,007
See, I don't think he does.

1104
00:59:49,907 --> 00:59:51,957
As long as I'm here, I'll wait.

1105
00:59:52,637 --> 00:59:55,447
Oh. I'm afraid Sir Alan isn't here.

1106
00:59:55,977 --> 00:59:57,446
Then I'll sit here until he is.

1107
00:59:57,447 --> 01:00:00,212
Perhaps you'd be
good enough to find me a chair.

1108
01:00:06,387 --> 01:00:08,116
Sir John, do we have a meeting?

1109
01:00:08,117 --> 01:00:10,386
We ought to have done long before this.

1110
01:00:10,387 --> 01:00:13,396
The 28 days you have to
appeal his sentence is running out.

1111
01:00:13,397 --> 01:00:15,626
Oh, look, I'm sorry I haven't
returned your calls.

1112
01:00:15,627 --> 01:00:18,166
The last few days
have been particularly hectic.

1113
01:00:18,167 --> 01:00:21,050
Your press interview created
a real hornet's nest.

1114
01:00:21,267 --> 01:00:22,637
Yes, it was meant to.

1115
01:00:22,897 --> 01:00:25,427
Yes, well, to be
absolutely honest with you,

1116
01:00:25,437 --> 01:00:28,336
I would prefer that this office and that
of the other Law Officers

1117
01:00:28,337 --> 01:00:29,917
be entirely independent,

1118
01:00:30,247 --> 01:00:31,647
but they're not.

1119
01:00:32,047 --> 01:00:35,007
And now we have to deal
with the political fallout.

1120
01:00:35,517 --> 01:00:38,341
You've sanctioned
a grave miscarriage of justice.

1121
01:00:38,687 --> 01:00:40,516
Or perhaps you were
one of its co<i>-</i>authors.

1122
01:00:40,517 --> 01:00:41,716
(CHUCKLING) I was no such thing.

1123
01:00:41,717 --> 01:00:43,186
Either way, the remedy is simple,

1124
01:00:43,187 --> 01:00:44,926
you must appeal Brooklands' sentence.

1125
01:00:44,927 --> 01:00:47,496
I might well have done.
But now, as a result of your actions,

1126
01:00:47,497 --> 01:00:49,397
the remedy is far from easy.

1127
01:00:51,027 --> 01:00:53,266
There isn't a single member
of the cabinet

1128
01:00:53,267 --> 01:00:55,577
who isn't calling for your resignation.

1129
01:00:55,997 --> 01:00:58,187
My advice to you, Sir John,

1130
01:00:59,207 --> 01:01:01,547
would be to lower your profile.

1131
01:01:02,037 --> 01:01:04,697
Yes. That still leaves
a grave injustice.

1132
01:01:05,877 --> 01:01:08,316
But then,
he is a big contributor to party funds.

1133
01:01:08,317 --> 01:01:10,657
This really is an abuse of privilege.

1134
01:01:11,287 --> 01:01:15,346
Either you begin the process to appeal
his ludicrously light sentence,

1135
01:01:15,557 --> 01:01:19,381
or I shall take up the invitation
I've received to speak at length

1136
01:01:19,657 --> 01:01:21,177
to Newsnight.

1137
01:01:22,827 --> 01:01:26,592
- Deed's gone completely mad, Joe.
- One always thought he would.

1138
01:01:26,967 --> 01:01:29,466
This has become
a personal vendetta for him.

1139
01:01:29,467 --> 01:01:31,866
Deed seems to imagine
that he's the only man in England

1140
01:01:31,867 --> 01:01:33,632
capable of delivering justice.

1141
01:01:33,637 --> 01:01:36,755
Perhaps one did lean towards leniency
with Brooklands,

1142
01:01:37,047 --> 01:01:38,400
but it was an accident.

1143
01:01:38,677 --> 01:01:40,325
The man's learnt his lesson.

1144
01:01:40,347 --> 01:01:43,347
You know, a prison sentence
would've been pointless.

1145
01:01:43,387 --> 01:01:45,386
However, whatever the rights
or the wrongs of the case,

1146
01:01:45,387 --> 01:01:47,697
one does not criticise a brother judge.

1147
01:01:48,117 --> 01:01:49,577
It's entirely bad form.

1148
01:01:49,857 --> 01:01:52,622
Mind you,
I would've given Brooklands six years.

1149
01:01:52,787 --> 01:01:54,627
Thank you for your support.

1150
01:01:55,157 --> 01:01:57,147
Oh, did you see these?

1151
01:01:59,967 --> 01:02:01,637
It's Deed and Mills

1152
01:02:01,937 --> 01:02:04,643
just before
she spent the night at his lodgings

1153
01:02:04,707 --> 01:02:06,847
last year, when she was before him.

1154
01:02:07,237 --> 01:02:09,876
Does it look like
they're not having an affair?

1155
01:02:09,877 --> 01:02:11,367
It does not.

1156
01:02:11,677 --> 01:02:13,987
These were taken at the lodgings.

1157
01:02:14,447 --> 01:02:17,216
Deed not only denies that they
were having an affair,

1158
01:02:17,217 --> 01:02:19,865
but he says that
they weren't in bed together.

1159
01:02:20,717 --> 01:02:23,556
Ian Rochester proposes
releasing them to the press

1160
01:02:23,557 --> 01:02:24,756
as a counter<i>-</i>offensive.

1161
01:02:24,757 --> 01:02:28,567
No, no, no. That would be doing
what Deed himself has been doing.

1162
01:02:29,897 --> 01:02:31,737
In a good cause, Joe.

1163
01:02:35,037 --> 01:02:36,087
Maybe.

1164
01:02:45,107 --> 01:02:46,807
- Ah.
- Joe.

1165
01:02:47,647 --> 01:02:50,186
You're about early.
Do you want some breakfast?

1166
01:02:50,187 --> 01:02:52,587
It was never a meal that interested me.

1167
01:02:53,187 --> 01:02:54,737
Some coffee perhaps?

1168
01:03:01,327 --> 01:03:03,547
You here to see me or Monty Everard?

1169
01:03:04,097 --> 01:03:05,396
He doesn't sleep here, you know.

1170
01:03:05,397 --> 01:03:07,866
I'm surprised you can bear
to be in the same building.

1171
01:03:07,867 --> 01:03:09,926
Well, he has asked that I be moved.

1172
01:03:10,067 --> 01:03:14,137
He's more inclined to have you removed
from the bench altogether.

1173
01:03:15,377 --> 01:03:17,966
Your public utterances
have been disgraceful.

1174
01:03:18,377 --> 01:03:20,576
I doubt if the public
would agree with you.

1175
01:03:20,577 --> 01:03:24,186
We don't have trial by mob, thank God,
but between the two of you,

1176
01:03:24,187 --> 01:03:26,586
you seem to be trying to
take what was, hitherto,

1177
01:03:26,587 --> 01:03:29,337
the preserve of gentlemen

1178
01:03:29,787 --> 01:03:31,217
into the gutter.

1179
01:03:31,657 --> 01:03:34,456
Does it really matter,
as long as justice is delivered?

1180
01:03:34,457 --> 01:03:35,797
It certainly does.

1181
01:03:36,057 --> 01:03:38,666
Otherwise, we'd simply have people
hacking each other to death

1182
01:03:38,667 --> 01:03:40,138
and calling it "justice".

1183
01:03:41,597 --> 01:03:42,907
Which is why

1184
01:03:43,707 --> 01:03:45,487
I will not condone

1185
01:03:46,107 --> 01:03:47,687
the release of these

1186
01:03:48,707 --> 01:03:49,967
to the press.

1187
01:03:57,747 --> 01:03:59,159
Where did you get these?

1188
01:03:59,417 --> 01:04:01,087
Does it really matter?

1189
01:04:01,387 --> 01:04:04,167
Ian Rochester proposes releasing them,

1190
01:04:04,727 --> 01:04:07,247
and my disapproval will not prevent it.

1191
01:04:12,967 --> 01:04:16,296
- Thanks for the warning, Joe.
- I didn't do it for your sake.

1192
01:04:16,297 --> 01:04:20,121
The only way you can avoid bringing
the bench into total disrepute

1193
01:04:20,677 --> 01:04:23,897
is for you to apologise
to Monty Everard, and then

1194
01:04:25,507 --> 01:04:26,507
to resign.

1195
01:04:26,647 --> 01:04:27,647
No.

1196
01:04:28,117 --> 01:04:30,727
Not as long as
Brooklands remains at liberty.

1197
01:04:33,187 --> 01:04:34,887
Then the consequences

1198
01:04:36,357 --> 01:04:38,027
will be unspeakable.

1199
01:04:44,997 --> 01:04:46,137
Mrs Dobbs,

1200
01:04:47,367 --> 01:04:50,838
the relationship you described
as existing with your husband

1201
01:04:52,107 --> 01:04:54,217
is far from the truth, is it not?

1202
01:04:55,077 --> 01:04:56,807
It's exactly as it was.

1203
01:04:57,347 --> 01:04:59,717
Hostile, accusatory.

1204
01:05:01,677 --> 01:05:05,089
A very different picture emerged
from the police inquiries.

1205
01:05:06,117 --> 01:05:08,487
You were a quiet couple, were you not?

1206
01:05:09,917 --> 01:05:11,317
Yes, sometimes.

1207
01:05:12,757 --> 01:05:14,747
But, mostly, it was like I said.

1208
01:05:15,927 --> 01:05:18,751
Why is it outsiders
saw only a mild<i>-</i>mannered man?

1209
01:05:19,627 --> 01:05:21,357
Always courteous and polite.

1210
01:05:21,667 --> 01:05:23,247
Alec was clever,

1211
01:05:24,137 --> 01:05:25,477
sly in his ways.

1212
01:05:25,737 --> 01:05:27,757
And never once got caught out?

1213
01:05:28,607 --> 01:05:31,677
His sort pour their venom
on those closest to them,

1214
01:05:32,677 --> 01:05:34,854
the ones that can do little about it.

1215
01:05:34,917 --> 01:05:37,859
What you're describing, Mrs Dobbs,
is a psychopath.

1216
01:05:39,747 --> 01:05:43,689
Nothing in your husband's medical
records indicates such tendencies.

1217
01:05:45,327 --> 01:05:48,667
Are you saying
the psychiatrists missed this?

1218
01:05:50,327 --> 01:05:52,727
(SIGHING) Alec could fool most people

1219
01:05:54,167 --> 01:05:57,887
with that charming,
wouldn't<i>-</i>hurt<i>-</i>a<i>-</i>fly manner of his.

1220
01:05:59,307 --> 01:06:01,707
You're honestly expecting this court

1221
01:06:02,107 --> 01:06:04,947
to believe that no one else
saw that in him, hmm?

1222
01:06:06,877 --> 01:06:09,837
You had to live with him
to know how clever he was.

1223
01:06:11,817 --> 01:06:15,507
I know he couldn't help it.
It was his illness.

1224
01:06:17,087 --> 01:06:20,717
But he was such a master of deception.

1225
01:06:23,567 --> 01:06:26,057
Oh, he knew how
to be charming, all right,

1226
01:06:27,197 --> 01:06:30,567
until we closed that front door,
and then I would get itl

1227
01:06:33,737 --> 01:06:34,737
(CRYING)

1228
01:06:39,047 --> 01:06:41,767
Mr Usher, may I have
the baseball bat, please?

1229
01:06:43,547 --> 01:06:45,977
Out of the container, if nobody objects.

1230
01:06:52,387 --> 01:06:55,270
Mrs Dobbs, this might be
very distressing for you,

1231
01:06:55,727 --> 01:06:57,897
but would you mind holding the bat?

1232
01:06:59,097 --> 01:07:00,297
Would you?

1233
01:07:14,477 --> 01:07:18,066
Now, would you swing it
as you used it to subdue your husband?

1234
01:07:25,127 --> 01:07:26,207
(GRUNTS)

1235
01:07:32,497 --> 01:07:34,615
Do you think the blow was like that?

1236
01:07:34,667 --> 01:07:36,097
Harder, I'd say.

1237
01:07:39,207 --> 01:07:42,678
Yes, it must be very upsetting for you.
Thank you, Mr Usher.

1238
01:07:45,947 --> 01:07:49,107
Well, this does seem
like a convenient moment.

1239
01:07:50,387 --> 01:07:52,886
I must remind the jury not to
discuss this case

1240
01:07:52,887 --> 01:07:56,240
other than when all together
until the morning. Thank you.

1241
01:08:00,187 --> 01:08:01,737
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

1242
01:08:02,027 --> 01:08:03,167
Come in.

1243
01:08:05,497 --> 01:08:07,777
A mysterious man just handed this in.

1244
01:08:09,797 --> 01:08:11,467
Hope it's not a bomb, Jo.

1245
01:08:11,807 --> 01:08:13,677
Might liven things up a bit.

1246
01:08:18,547 --> 01:08:19,857
You okay, Jo?

1247
01:08:20,307 --> 01:08:21,737
Yes, I'm fine.

1248
01:08:41,667 --> 01:08:43,887
Mrs Mills is at the door, my lord.

1249
01:08:44,437 --> 01:08:45,506
Bring her in, then.

1250
01:08:45,507 --> 01:08:48,155
She doesn't wish to
enter the house, Sir John.

1251
01:08:49,607 --> 01:08:50,917
She doesn't.

1252
01:08:58,247 --> 01:09:00,587
Jo, why won't you come in?

1253
01:09:05,487 --> 01:09:07,126
They arrived at chambers a while ago.

1254
01:09:07,127 --> 01:09:10,422
If you don't stop this,
someone is going to publish them.

1255
01:09:11,167 --> 01:09:12,866
You know where
they came from, don't you?

1256
01:09:12,867 --> 01:09:14,297
Getting any work?

1257
01:09:15,037 --> 01:09:17,332
Empty promises from clerks, that's all.

1258
01:09:17,437 --> 01:09:20,085
I have a feeling that
might change quite soon.

1259
01:09:20,437 --> 01:09:22,247
What are you going to do?

1260
01:09:22,637 --> 01:09:23,637
Oh, no.

1261
01:09:23,847 --> 01:09:25,546
Don't do anything
that would make this worse.

1262
01:09:25,547 --> 01:09:27,136
How can I make things worse

1263
01:09:27,247 --> 01:09:29,746
than a bunch of cowards getting at you
to make me stop?

1264
01:09:29,747 --> 01:09:31,286
Why would I accede to that?

1265
01:09:31,287 --> 01:09:33,307
Because I want you to.

1266
01:09:34,557 --> 01:09:36,322
Why can't you let this one go?

1267
01:09:36,617 --> 01:09:37,756
You don't mean that, Jo.

1268
01:09:37,757 --> 01:09:38,837
I do.

1269
01:09:40,057 --> 01:09:42,487
I will marry you
if that's what you want,

1270
01:09:44,067 --> 01:09:45,997
but it's me or this fight.

1271
01:09:51,767 --> 01:09:53,415
Oh, am I getting an apology?

1272
01:09:53,637 --> 01:09:56,406
I've sent copies of these
to the Attorney General.

1273
01:09:56,407 --> 01:09:58,946
He'd better agree
to appeal Brooklands's sentence.

1274
01:09:58,947 --> 01:10:00,177
If he doesn't,

1275
01:10:00,447 --> 01:10:02,476
then I'll contact the <i>'Times'</i> again

1276
01:10:02,477 --> 01:10:06,116
and explain how you're using
paparazzi shots to force me to resign.

1277
01:10:06,117 --> 01:10:08,286
You might just overreach yourself, sir.

1278
01:10:08,287 --> 01:10:11,326
And tell the Attorney and Sir Ian to
lay off Jo Mills while you're at it.

1279
01:10:11,327 --> 01:10:12,967
This is preposterousl

1280
01:10:24,837 --> 01:10:26,166
MONTY: The whole notion's
utterly offensive.

1281
01:10:26,167 --> 01:10:27,226
(KNOCKING ON DOOR)

1282
01:10:27,277 --> 01:10:28,647
Come in.

1283
01:10:28,907 --> 01:10:30,476
We're sorry we're late, Sir Ian.

1284
01:10:30,477 --> 01:10:32,087
No matter, Laurence.

1285
01:10:34,517 --> 01:10:37,459
You know Laurence James,
our circuit administrator.

1286
01:10:37,717 --> 01:10:39,586
Perhaps you'd take a note, Laurence.

1287
01:10:39,587 --> 01:10:41,807
Civil service habit, Monty.

1288
01:10:45,327 --> 01:10:46,726
My position doesn't change.

1289
01:10:46,727 --> 01:10:49,627
It would be foolish to
accede to Deed's demands.

1290
01:10:50,127 --> 01:10:53,266
Well, it seems you may have misjudged
the public mood, Monty.

1291
01:10:53,267 --> 01:10:56,766
I sentenced Brooklands as I would have
anyone on such a similar charge.

1292
01:10:56,767 --> 01:10:59,606
The decision to appeal the sentence
is for you, Alan,

1293
01:10:59,607 --> 01:11:01,431
but with Deed pushing as he is,

1294
01:11:01,707 --> 01:11:03,707
the bad press isn't gonna go away.

1295
01:11:04,007 --> 01:11:07,243
He'll drag in everyone,
and we know who'll be the loser.

1296
01:11:07,917 --> 01:11:09,546
Is there any truth to his allegations?

1297
01:11:09,547 --> 01:11:11,959
It matters not a jot
if there is or isn't.

1298
01:11:12,347 --> 01:11:15,347
The collateral damage increases
the longer we delay.

1299
01:11:16,317 --> 01:11:19,906
Yes, well, I suppose an appeal is
the way it has to go, Monty.

1300
01:11:20,757 --> 01:11:23,228
I'm to be led
like a lamb to the slaughter?

1301
01:11:23,627 --> 01:11:24,857
Hardly.

1302
01:11:25,767 --> 01:11:27,297
Who would hear the appeal?

1303
01:11:33,907 --> 01:11:35,637
Why not Mr Justice Deed?

1304
01:11:36,877 --> 01:11:40,276
Oh, don't be fatuous, Mr James.
He's not an appeal court judge.

1305
01:11:40,277 --> 01:11:42,983
Deed could sit as a winger
in the appeal court.

1306
01:11:46,347 --> 01:11:47,456
What's your thinking, Laurence?

1307
01:11:47,457 --> 01:11:49,516
Well, sir, if Deed heard this case,

1308
01:11:49,887 --> 01:11:52,358
he's so incandescent
about the whole issue,

1309
01:11:52,427 --> 01:11:55,156
he's bound to come down
too hard on the appellant.

1310
01:11:55,157 --> 01:11:57,126
Brooklands will then have
righteous ground

1311
01:11:57,127 --> 01:11:59,527
for a second appeal against sentence.

1312
01:11:59,967 --> 01:12:00,967
Hmm.

1313
01:12:02,097 --> 01:12:03,467
It has merit, Monty.

1314
01:12:03,767 --> 01:12:06,566
And that would square the circle
for the cabinet and political opinion,

1315
01:12:06,567 --> 01:12:08,787
and leave Deed wrong<i>-</i>footed.

1316
01:12:16,777 --> 01:12:18,857
I won't go along with it, Clive.

1317
01:12:19,247 --> 01:12:20,656
They can't force me
to stand trial again.

1318
01:12:20,657 --> 01:12:22,117
They can and they are.

1319
01:12:22,387 --> 01:12:23,986
They're appealing your sentence.

1320
01:12:23,987 --> 01:12:26,929
It's thought to be so light
as to diminish the law.

1321
01:12:28,797 --> 01:12:31,680
Well, I shall...
I shall simply leave the country.

1322
01:12:32,167 --> 01:12:34,977
James, they're relying
on us to play ball.

1323
01:12:35,437 --> 01:12:37,566
That's why we got the call
from the Attorney General's office

1324
01:12:37,567 --> 01:12:39,087
to come in for a chat.

1325
01:12:48,517 --> 01:12:49,567
Andy,

1326
01:12:50,787 --> 01:12:54,670
I want you to think very carefully
about what I'm going to ask you.

1327
01:12:56,087 --> 01:12:57,787
You told defence counsel

1328
01:12:58,227 --> 01:13:00,777
your father was attacking
first your mother,

1329
01:13:01,757 --> 01:13:02,897
then yourself.

1330
01:13:03,157 --> 01:13:05,267
Is that absolutely true?

1331
01:13:08,167 --> 01:13:10,627
I know you're close
to your mother, Andy,

1332
01:13:11,607 --> 01:13:13,447
but she can't help you now.

1333
01:13:15,807 --> 01:13:17,067
Yes, it...

1334
01:13:18,107 --> 01:13:20,037
It was the truth.

1335
01:13:20,407 --> 01:13:23,525
And if I were to tell you
that forensic experts stated

1336
01:13:23,947 --> 01:13:25,586
that your father's blood indicated

1337
01:13:25,587 --> 01:13:27,807
that he couldn't have been fighting,

1338
01:13:28,187 --> 01:13:29,516
that he was probably asleep...

1339
01:13:29,517 --> 01:13:30,517
No.

1340
01:13:32,427 --> 01:13:34,157
It happened like I said.

1341
01:13:36,527 --> 01:13:38,017
How was that again?

1342
01:13:39,767 --> 01:13:42,457
Dad was very angry and...

1343
01:13:43,397 --> 01:13:45,297
And shouting at Mum when I...

1344
01:13:45,637 --> 01:13:48,755
When I tried to stop him,
he attacked me and then Jan.

1345
01:13:48,837 --> 01:13:51,737
He attacked you, then your mother?

1346
01:13:52,947 --> 01:13:55,359
That's different to
what you told defence.

1347
01:13:55,417 --> 01:13:56,937
Which was it?

1348
01:13:57,247 --> 01:13:58,917
I'm not sure.

1349
01:14:00,547 --> 01:14:02,157
He was shouting and...

1350
01:14:02,957 --> 01:14:04,605
And calling us filthy names.

1351
01:14:04,827 --> 01:14:05,827
Ah.

1352
01:14:06,357 --> 01:14:09,397
What names did he call you?

1353
01:14:11,027 --> 01:14:14,027
- Don't remember.
- I think you do, Andy, don't you?

1354
01:14:14,267 --> 01:14:15,757
Son of a serpent.

1355
01:14:16,437 --> 01:14:18,085
Was that what he called you?

1356
01:14:18,237 --> 01:14:19,817
- Son of a serpent?
- No.

1357
01:14:20,467 --> 01:14:23,409
Ah, then perhaps he was
talking in his sleep, then?

1358
01:14:23,737 --> 01:14:26,167
I mean, he was asleep

1359
01:14:27,007 --> 01:14:28,346
when she hit him, wasn't he?

1360
01:14:28,347 --> 01:14:29,837
No, she didn't...

1361
01:14:31,547 --> 01:14:34,386
- He wasn't.
- You're covering up for her, aren't you?

1362
01:14:34,387 --> 01:14:37,377
This poor, drug<i>-</i>subdued man,
your father...

1363
01:14:37,887 --> 01:14:39,847
No, he was angry. He wasl

1364
01:14:40,187 --> 01:14:41,917
That's simply not true.

1365
01:14:42,497 --> 01:14:45,615
He'd fallen asleep from his drugs,
she crept up on him

1366
01:14:45,667 --> 01:14:47,127
- and hit him.
- Nol

1367
01:14:47,867 --> 01:14:49,136
- No...
- She did, didn't she?

1368
01:14:49,137 --> 01:14:50,137
Nol

1369
01:14:50,807 --> 01:14:52,007
He found us in bed.

1370
01:14:52,237 --> 01:14:53,527
What?

1371
01:14:56,277 --> 01:14:58,087
I mean, in the bedroom, we...

1372
01:14:58,447 --> 01:15:00,406
- We were hiding.
- That's not what you said.

1373
01:15:00,407 --> 01:15:01,667
- Yeah...
- Nol

1374
01:15:03,047 --> 01:15:04,887
You said "in bed".

1375
01:15:05,247 --> 01:15:07,907
I meant "in the bedroom".

1376
01:15:10,387 --> 01:15:11,617
Andy,

1377
01:15:15,197 --> 01:15:17,417
your father wrote in his diary

1378
01:15:18,367 --> 01:15:21,587
that you were having
sexual relations with your mother.

1379
01:15:23,797 --> 01:15:25,966
And he wasn't being paranoid, was he?

1380
01:15:25,967 --> 01:15:26,967
He was.

1381
01:15:27,167 --> 01:15:28,167
Mrs Dobbs.

1382
01:15:28,237 --> 01:15:30,427
My lord, this is undue intervention.

1383
01:15:30,847 --> 01:15:32,397
I... We were...

1384
01:15:33,007 --> 01:15:34,707
In the bedrooml

1385
01:15:35,047 --> 01:15:37,627
It wasn't just a suspicion.

1386
01:15:39,117 --> 01:15:40,577
He made several entries.

1387
01:15:40,887 --> 01:15:43,829
- He was sickl
- Mrs Dobbs, you will remain silent.

1388
01:15:47,727 --> 01:15:50,337
Andy, how many times

1389
01:15:51,427 --> 01:15:53,369
were you in bed with your mother?

1390
01:15:55,637 --> 01:15:57,157
He found you, didn't he?

1391
01:15:57,437 --> 01:15:59,143
No, he didn't. No, he didn't.

1392
01:16:02,977 --> 01:16:04,027
(SIGHS)

1393
01:16:05,007 --> 01:16:08,257
Well, on the 9th of January,

1394
01:16:10,847 --> 01:16:11,957
he wrote,

1395
01:16:13,647 --> 01:16:17,657
"Spoke to Father Cattle
about A and J in her bed.

1396
01:16:21,157 --> 01:16:22,937
"He agrees it's evil,

1397
01:16:24,197 --> 01:16:26,477
"suggests I confront J."

1398
01:16:29,167 --> 01:16:30,796
Did he confront your mother?

1399
01:16:30,797 --> 01:16:31,877
Nol

1400
01:16:34,277 --> 01:16:35,857
I don't know.

1401
01:16:38,707 --> 01:16:41,517
But he did find you in bed
with your mother

1402
01:16:42,277 --> 01:16:44,007
on the day that he died.

1403
01:16:44,887 --> 01:16:46,027
Didn't he?

1404
01:17:06,207 --> 01:17:08,678
She's guilty,
there's no question about it.

1405
01:17:09,037 --> 01:17:10,407
(ALL CHATTERING)

1406
01:17:10,677 --> 01:17:12,276
MAN: Of course she's guilty.

1407
01:17:12,277 --> 01:17:13,647
Come on, wait a moment.

1408
01:17:13,907 --> 01:17:17,476
Even if she was abusing her son,
it doesn't make her a murderer.

1409
01:17:17,477 --> 01:17:19,647
It does give her a motive, though.

1410
01:17:20,047 --> 01:17:23,989
Well, how many of you thought
she wasn't guilty up until that point?

1411
01:17:25,927 --> 01:17:28,296
Well, no one's gonna
convince me she's innocent.

1412
01:17:28,297 --> 01:17:29,496
Doing that to her son.

1413
01:17:29,497 --> 01:17:31,607
Doing what? What was she doing?

1414
01:17:32,467 --> 01:17:35,409
In bed with him.
What do you think they were doing?

1415
01:17:35,837 --> 01:17:36,837
It's disgustingl

1416
01:17:37,037 --> 01:17:39,266
Look, lots of boys get into bed
with their mums.

1417
01:17:39,267 --> 01:17:40,566
(SCOFFING) At that age?

1418
01:17:40,567 --> 01:17:42,347
I'd lock him up, too.

1419
01:17:43,237 --> 01:17:45,676
Knew there was something weird
about those two.

1420
01:17:45,677 --> 01:17:47,746
Wouldn't be surprised
if the boy did the killing.

1421
01:17:47,747 --> 01:17:49,516
- He's a weirdo.
- That's ridiculous.

1422
01:17:49,517 --> 01:17:51,097
Why don't we take a vote?

1423
01:17:51,417 --> 01:17:53,146
All those who say the lady's guilty...

1424
01:17:53,147 --> 01:17:54,556
MAN 1: Yes, absolutely.
WOMAN: Yeah, I agree.

1425
01:17:54,557 --> 01:17:56,087
MAN 2: Yeah.
MAN 3: Guilty.

1426
01:18:00,387 --> 01:18:03,326
Hey, we're almost there.
We can almost go back in with it.

1427
01:18:03,327 --> 01:18:04,696
We've got to discuss
this some more.

1428
01:18:04,697 --> 01:18:07,227
Forget it.
You're out<i>-</i>voted by three to one.

1429
01:18:07,367 --> 01:18:11,536
It's murder. I mean, doesn't that mean
that it has to be a unanimous verdict?

1430
01:18:11,537 --> 01:18:12,617
Uh...

1431
01:18:13,437 --> 01:18:15,717
Well, all those for not guilty.

1432
01:18:21,247 --> 01:18:24,071
She admitted to hitting him.
She wasn't provoked.

1433
01:18:24,487 --> 01:18:26,716
She wanted to be alone
with her weirdo kid.

1434
01:18:26,717 --> 01:18:28,617
I think you're right.

1435
01:18:28,957 --> 01:18:31,722
Let's look through what evidence
we've got, hmm?

1436
01:18:41,967 --> 01:18:43,717
And not guilty.

1437
01:18:47,207 --> 01:18:50,376
You'll never convince me
she didn't want her husband dead.

1438
01:18:50,377 --> 01:18:53,436
The evidence is clear.
She was sleeping with her son.

1439
01:18:54,617 --> 01:18:56,846
Doesn't exactly sound
like she was innocent.

1440
01:18:56,847 --> 01:18:57,897
What if

1441
01:18:58,617 --> 01:19:02,017
we look at the boy
maybe killing his father?

1442
01:19:02,557 --> 01:19:05,386
- Now you're talking.
- They're not on trial for incest,

1443
01:19:05,387 --> 01:19:07,926
if that's what it is,
it's her who's on trial.

1444
01:19:07,927 --> 01:19:10,596
If the boy did the killing,
did the mother know?

1445
01:19:10,597 --> 01:19:11,950
How could she not know?

1446
01:19:12,127 --> 01:19:14,996
They know everything
in those sort of relationships.

1447
01:19:14,997 --> 01:19:16,821
They worm their way inside you,

1448
01:19:16,997 --> 01:19:18,606
into your brain so you don't
know what you're doing

1449
01:19:18,607 --> 01:19:20,387
or even saying half the time.

1450
01:19:20,707 --> 01:19:22,776
You feel like you're being smothered,
like you're sinking.

1451
01:19:22,777 --> 01:19:24,906
Well, you tell yourself it's all right,
you can get out,

1452
01:19:24,907 --> 01:19:26,247
but you can'tl

1453
01:19:34,457 --> 01:19:36,163
I mean, look at the evidence.

1454
01:19:36,387 --> 01:19:37,757
Are you stupid?

1455
01:19:39,057 --> 01:19:40,881
- Look at itl
- Yes, look at it.

1456
01:19:41,097 --> 01:19:43,326
The blow came from
a right<i>-</i>handed swing,

1457
01:19:43,327 --> 01:19:45,426
and the mother is clearly left<i>-</i>handed.

1458
01:19:45,427 --> 01:19:46,896
Look how she took the bat.

1459
01:19:46,897 --> 01:19:48,536
But the most important thing

1460
01:19:48,537 --> 01:19:51,436
is the fact that the dead man
had no adrenaline in his blood.

1461
01:19:51,437 --> 01:19:54,606
He wasn't fighting with that monster
he was married to, not then.

1462
01:19:54,607 --> 01:19:56,447
She planned and calculated,

1463
01:19:56,807 --> 01:19:59,631
first, how to kill him
and then cover her tracks.

1464
01:20:03,847 --> 01:20:05,446
That poor wet boy's part of it, too,

1465
01:20:05,447 --> 01:20:08,107
whether he did or not.
My money, he did.

1466
01:20:15,127 --> 01:20:16,557
Judge,

1467
01:20:16,827 --> 01:20:20,063
- the jury's coming back for a direction.
- Another one?

1468
01:20:22,937 --> 01:20:23,937
Thank you.

1469
01:20:31,107 --> 01:20:32,567
You want to know

1470
01:20:33,177 --> 01:20:35,883
if the defendant didn't swing
the baseball bat,

1471
01:20:36,047 --> 01:20:37,977
would she still be guilty?

1472
01:20:39,317 --> 01:20:41,386
Well, she might be
guilty of a lot of things,

1473
01:20:41,387 --> 01:20:43,564
but she wouldn't be guilty of murder.

1474
01:20:54,597 --> 01:20:56,950
The jury appears
to be having difficulty.

1475
01:20:57,337 --> 01:20:59,926
Are you here to sell
the abolition of juries?

1476
01:21:00,167 --> 01:21:01,462
I'm not in the market.

1477
01:21:01,477 --> 01:21:05,125
As I told Mrs Cooper, it's something
altogether more agreeable.

1478
01:21:05,477 --> 01:21:09,536
The Attorney General is going to appeal
the James Brooklands sentence.

1479
01:21:11,047 --> 01:21:12,857
Without strings or impediment?

1480
01:21:13,217 --> 01:21:15,387
You're far too suspicious, my lord.

1481
01:21:16,587 --> 01:21:20,235
Coop, could you give Mr Dobbs's diary
to Chief Inspector Hurst?

1482
01:21:20,757 --> 01:21:22,463
Ask her to interview the boy.

1483
01:21:24,297 --> 01:21:26,107
An interesting development?

1484
01:21:27,127 --> 01:21:29,566
I think this jury's going to
find her not guilty.

1485
01:21:29,567 --> 01:21:30,567
Ah.

1486
01:21:35,707 --> 01:21:38,506
Everyone from the Lord Chancellor
to the Deputy Prime Minister

1487
01:21:38,507 --> 01:21:42,096
is breathing a sigh of relief
about Brooklands being appealed.

1488
01:21:44,077 --> 01:21:47,707
The Lord Chancellor would like you
to sit on the appeal.

1489
01:21:52,057 --> 01:21:54,881
Does this presage a place
on the Appellate Bench?

1490
01:21:55,757 --> 01:21:57,307
We'll take a view.

1491
01:21:59,697 --> 01:22:01,521
When would the appeal be heard?

1492
01:22:01,537 --> 01:22:02,997
The sooner, the better.

1493
01:22:04,067 --> 01:22:07,185
It's another six weeks
till you're back in the Strand.

1494
01:22:09,107 --> 01:22:12,776
You could hear it down here, so as not
to interfere with your sitting,

1495
01:22:12,777 --> 01:22:14,357
if you're agreeable.

1496
01:22:22,057 --> 01:22:24,646
I really don't
understand you any more, John.

1497
01:22:25,187 --> 01:22:27,786
You appear to have got what you wanted,
and yet,

1498
01:22:27,787 --> 01:22:28,956
still you're not happy.

1499
01:22:28,957 --> 01:22:31,887
I mean, without trying to
pre<i>-</i>empt your sentence,

1500
01:22:32,367 --> 01:22:35,485
I imagine that Brooklands
will find himself in prison.

1501
01:22:36,737 --> 01:22:39,006
It won't bring back
the two little girls and their mother,

1502
01:22:39,007 --> 01:22:41,236
but a lot of people will see
that justice is possible.

1503
01:22:41,237 --> 01:22:43,847
It's too easy. Do they think I'm stupid?

1504
01:22:47,007 --> 01:22:48,976
Is it just that you miss the fight?

1505
01:22:48,977 --> 01:22:50,746
Go back to being a QC, if it is.

1506
01:22:50,747 --> 01:22:51,747
(CHUCKLING)

1507
01:22:52,647 --> 01:22:54,047
What about you?

1508
01:22:54,447 --> 01:22:55,977
Any signs of any work yet?

1509
01:22:56,087 --> 01:22:58,107
There have been a few inquiries.

1510
01:22:58,957 --> 01:23:00,086
Think about it objectively.

1511
01:23:00,087 --> 01:23:03,596
What have I done to deserve
this kind of reward from the LCD?

1512
01:23:03,597 --> 01:23:05,026
You stood up to the bullies.

1513
01:23:05,027 --> 01:23:06,337
Judgel

1514
01:23:07,027 --> 01:23:08,851
The jury's back with a verdict.

1515
01:23:20,847 --> 01:23:23,200
HARRISON: Will the foreman please stand?

1516
01:23:24,347 --> 01:23:27,642
Have you reached a verdict
upon which you are all agreed?

1517
01:23:27,687 --> 01:23:28,947
Yes, we have.

1518
01:23:34,657 --> 01:23:37,726
On the count of murder,
how do you find the defendant?

1519
01:23:37,727 --> 01:23:39,157
Guilty or not guilty?

1520
01:23:39,427 --> 01:23:40,477
Guilty.

1521
01:23:41,737 --> 01:23:43,167
(JURY MURMURING)

1522
01:23:43,437 --> 01:23:45,379
MAN: We never agreed that at alll

1523
01:23:45,637 --> 01:23:47,387
- No, it was mel
- Andy, no.

1524
01:23:47,737 --> 01:23:49,517
(JURY CLAMOURING)

1525
01:23:49,837 --> 01:23:52,602
We said not guilty.
We all agreed on not guilty.

1526
01:23:52,777 --> 01:23:53,887
Silencel

1527
01:23:56,577 --> 01:23:57,837
Is this true?

1528
01:23:58,487 --> 01:24:00,097
- Yes.
- It's not guilty.

1529
01:24:00,817 --> 01:24:01,817
Yes.

1530
01:24:04,187 --> 01:24:06,707
Is that the true verdict of you all?

1531
01:24:07,127 --> 01:24:08,297
ALL: Yes.

1532
01:24:10,197 --> 01:24:12,217
I made a mistake.

1533
01:24:14,497 --> 01:24:16,427
I was just so tense and nervous.

1534
01:24:18,037 --> 01:24:20,257
Then I'll accept the true verdict.

1535
01:24:20,807 --> 01:24:24,937
Mrs Dobbs you have been found
not guilty of murder.

1536
01:24:25,607 --> 01:24:27,357
You are free to go.

1537
01:24:30,647 --> 01:24:31,647
USHER: All rise.

1538
01:24:31,717 --> 01:24:32,857
- Andy?
- Mum.

1539
01:24:33,087 --> 01:24:34,558
Where are you taking him?

1540
01:24:34,817 --> 01:24:35,817
Muml Muml

1541
01:24:36,057 --> 01:24:37,469
JAN: You can't take himl

1542
01:25:00,977 --> 01:25:02,467
(ALL CLAMOURING)

1543
01:25:02,817 --> 01:25:04,316
REPORTER: Would you like to
say anything at all?

1544
01:25:04,317 --> 01:25:06,435
JAMES: Excuse me, please. Thank you.

1545
01:25:10,857 --> 01:25:13,093
God, I thought all this was behind me.

1546
01:25:13,257 --> 01:25:14,586
It soon will be, James.

1547
01:25:14,587 --> 01:25:18,426
It's all right for you, Clive.
You get paid whatever happens to me.

1548
01:25:18,427 --> 01:25:21,936
Having heard all the arguments in
this appeal, Mr Brooklands,

1549
01:25:21,937 --> 01:25:24,996
we would ordinarily reserve judgment
to a later date.

1550
01:25:26,207 --> 01:25:29,897
But because there is such
public concern over this case,

1551
01:25:31,007 --> 01:25:35,017
and, regrettably, judges can no longer
ignore public sentiment,

1552
01:25:35,647 --> 01:25:39,077
we are going to retire to
consider our judgment.

1553
01:25:40,747 --> 01:25:42,147
USHER: All rise.

1554
01:25:45,057 --> 01:25:48,587
You know, I think a very stiff
prison sentence is called for.

1555
01:25:49,127 --> 01:25:50,892
Ten years wouldn't come amiss.

1556
01:25:51,157 --> 01:25:53,628
Can't we be
a little more imaginative, Joe?

1557
01:25:53,727 --> 01:25:55,496
The public would expect that sentence.

1558
01:25:55,497 --> 01:25:57,436
- Yes.
- Between what they might expect

1559
01:25:57,437 --> 01:26:00,366
and what we might give him,
there is a yawning gap.

1560
01:26:00,367 --> 01:26:02,447
I don't see that there need be.

1561
01:26:03,007 --> 01:26:05,976
Well, personally, I'd give him
two years on bread and water,

1562
01:26:05,977 --> 01:26:08,557
150 lashes and transportation
to Australia.

1563
01:26:09,377 --> 01:26:10,846
But then, I am new to this.

1564
01:26:10,847 --> 01:26:14,200
Well, you're going to make
a very splendid Lord of Appeal.

1565
01:26:15,487 --> 01:26:17,237
Pity those days are gone.

1566
01:26:17,917 --> 01:26:20,726
- Ten years, Michael?
- Yes, that'd be all right, Joe.

1567
01:26:20,727 --> 01:26:24,357
No, 10 is what he deserves,
five is what we should give him.

1568
01:26:24,927 --> 01:26:28,869
- He did think he would get none.
- Five. Is that your lucky number?

1569
01:26:29,337 --> 01:26:30,966
He'll spend one year
in a proper prison,

1570
01:26:30,967 --> 01:26:33,406
two and a half in an open prison,
and be off.

1571
01:26:33,407 --> 01:26:36,576
And with 10, he'll be back here
on appeal and out in five minutes,

1572
01:26:36,577 --> 01:26:37,977
and you know it.

1573
01:26:41,307 --> 01:26:43,131
This court has decided to allow

1574
01:26:43,317 --> 01:26:47,297
the Attorney General's appeal
against your over<i>-</i>lenient sentence.

1575
01:26:47,917 --> 01:26:51,377
You will, therefore,
go to prison for six years.

1576
01:26:53,387 --> 01:26:54,387
(GASPS)

1577
01:26:54,557 --> 01:26:56,596
Although it is a matter
for the prison service,

1578
01:26:56,597 --> 01:27:00,539
we recommend to the Director General
that your time should be served

1579
01:27:00,727 --> 01:27:02,217
in a secure prison,

1580
01:27:02,997 --> 01:27:07,717
one from which you cannot conduct
the daily intercourse of your business.

1581
01:27:08,437 --> 01:27:10,807
No, that's not fair. They promised me...

1582
01:27:12,807 --> 01:27:14,690
I'll talk to the Home Secretary.

1583
01:27:14,847 --> 01:27:17,597
I'm sure you'll find him most receptive.

1584
01:27:19,017 --> 01:27:20,307
Take him down.

1585
01:27:25,727 --> 01:27:26,987
I can walkl

1586
01:27:27,657 --> 01:27:29,267
I can walk by myselfl

1587
01:27:31,357 --> 01:27:33,577
Why only six, John? He deserved more.

1588
01:27:33,967 --> 01:27:35,717
Six is my lucky number.

1589
01:27:37,237 --> 01:27:39,106
Do you realise,
since I've been having therapy,

1590
01:27:39,107 --> 01:27:41,006
I haven't propositioned you once?

1591
01:27:41,007 --> 01:27:42,737
Hmm. You're inching forward.

1592
01:27:43,937 --> 01:27:45,687
The foundations are going in.

1593
01:27:46,007 --> 01:27:49,537
What are you looking for,
a Scout's badge for responsibility?

1594
01:27:49,917 --> 01:27:51,227
Is there one?

1595
01:27:51,647 --> 01:27:54,295
We'll have to have one created
in your honour.

1596
01:27:54,747 --> 01:27:56,057
Dyb, dyb, dyb.

1597
01:28:01,697 --> 01:28:02,927
(WHINING)

1598
01:28:03,227 --> 01:28:09,927
